// front::types_expr: the type of an expression, up and down (#1112, ticket J1b of #1220). Bidirectional and local, with
// no Hindley-Milner: `infer` says what an expression is from its parts, `check` says it against a type that the
// context expects, and the expected type flows down into what needs it (a literal, `{...}`, a lambda, `.none`, a
// designated initializer) while the other types flow up. Built on front::types (J1a); the conversion rules are J1c's
// (front/types_convert.smith) and the declarations, statements and bodies J1d's. Not wired into `smith cc`.
//
// THE API
//
//   infer(c, e)            The type of `e`, from `e` alone. Every expression gets its type recorded with
//                          `types::set_type`, the sub-expressions too. An error is reported with `types::fail` and the
//                          type is `c->table.invalid`, which makes the expressions around it silent (one mistake, one
//                          report).
//   check(c, e, expected)  The type of `e` where `expected` is wanted. `expected` flows down: an unsuffixed literal
//                          takes it when it fits (`U8 small = 200;`; `U8 big = 300;` is "300 does not fit U8 ..."), a
//                          `{...}` list, `.none`, `.red` and a lambda are typed by it, and a conditional gives it to
//                          its branches. What is left (an expression whose own type is not the expected one) goes
//                          through the conversion hook below. The type recorded for `e` is the type `e` has; the
//                          context converts it. `expected` null or `Auto` is `infer`.
//   set_hooks(Hooks)       The functions J1c and J1d wire in. Until they are set:
//                          - `convert(c, e, from, to)` (J1c, `types_convert::convert`) says whether a value of type
//                            `from` may stand where `to` is expected and reports the error itself when it may not.
//                            DEFAULT while unset (types_decl::install sets J1c's): exact type equality, plus `null`
//                            where a pointer is expected and a function where `Fn(F)` of its own type is. Nothing
//                            else converts: not integer widening, not a value to `Option(T)`'s `.some`, not an array
//                            to a slice.
//                          - `cast(c, e, from, to)` (J1c, `types_convert::cast_ok`) says whether the cast `e` may be
//                            written, reports the error itself when it may not and marks a cast that can lose a value
//                            (`types::mark_cast_checked`, read back with `types::cast_checked`). Unset: every cast is
//                            allowed and none is checked.
//                          - `lambda_body(c, lambda, type)` (J1d) checks the body of a lambda whose type `Fn(F)` was
//                            decided here (the parameters' types are the slots of `type->element`, the result its
//                            element). Unset, a lambda is reported as `not yet typed: a lambda body`.
//
// WHAT IS TYPED: integer, floating, character, string, boolean and null literals; names (looked up in
// `c->scopes`); `+ - ! ~ & *` and `++ --`; every binary operator (arithmetic, shifts, comparisons to Bool, bitwise,
// logical) and assignment, plain and compound; `.` and `->`; `a[i]` on arrays, slices, pointers and `Str`, and
// `a[i:j]`; calls (the result of the callee's function type, each argument checked against its parameter by the
// convert hook, the count checked); casts (the written type; whether the cast is allowed is the cast hook's, J1c);
// `sizeof`, `alignof`, `countof`; `c ? a : b`; `(T){...}` and `{...}`; lambdas with the types of an expected `Fn(F)`.
// VALUES (#1511, C2a, spec "Control flow" and "Types" of #1428): `if`, `if let`, `match` and a block used as a value
// (`ast::stmt_expr`): the last line of a block is its value, an `if` needs an `else` to be a value, and every branch
// has the one type (the expected one, else the first branch's). A branch that yields no value makes the whole `void`
// (a block with no last expression, an `if` with no `else`), and where a type is expected that is an error. `match` is
// exhaustive over an enumeration (a missing enumerator is listed) or `?T` (`.some(x)`, `.none`); `if let .some(x) = o`
// binds `x` in its `then`. A payload enumeration's `.circle(c)` binds `c` as what the enumerator carries. `.some(x)` is
// the value of a `?T`. The walk of a value statement pushes its expressions as frames of the work stack, between
// pseudo-frames that open and close scopes and bind names, and one last frame that joins the branches' types.
//
// CONSTRUCTION AND POINTERS (#1513, C2b, spec "Structs, enums and building values" and "Pointers"): `Point(x = 1)`
// (a call whose callee names a struct: positional fields in order, then named ones in any order; a field left out takes
// its default, and with none it is an error, #1847), named arguments of a function (`f(b = 1, a = 2)`, matched by the
// parameter names of its declaration), `[1, 2, 3]`, and, for a variable of smith's own syntax (`Symbol.is_new`):
// constant unless `mut`, `&x` is `*mut T` for a `mut x` and else `*T`, `p.x` through a pointer, and a write through
// `*T` is an error naming `*mut T`.
//
// NOT YET TYPED (each reported as `not yet typed: <kind>` with its span): `try`, a name from
// another module (`arena::push`), nested designators (`.a.b = 1`), a designator index that is not an integer literal.
//
// LITERALS NEXT TO A TYPED OPERAND: in `x + 1` and `x < 10` an unsuffixed literal takes the type of the other operand
// (checked to fit); two literals take the expected type of the whole (`U8 b = 1 + 2;`). Any other pair of different
// types is an error that says which cast to write. Strict like the rest of a module: `&&`, `||`, `!` and `?:` take
// Bool.
//
// No recursion: a work stack of frames (a node, the type expected of it, a stage); a node with children pushes itself
// at the next stage and then its children, and reads the children's types back from the side table. No malloc: the
// stack is in the current allocator (std::mem).
module front::types_expr;

import front::ast;
import front::parse as syntax;
import front::types;
import front::types_fn;
import std::arena;
import std::format;
import std::mem;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The hooks

export typedef Bool (*Convert)(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, types::TypeRef from, types::TypeRef to);
// Whether `(to)operand` may be written (J1c's `cast_ok`); reports its own error, and marks a cast that needs a run-time
// check with `types::mark_cast_checked`. `e` is the cast.
export typedef Bool (*CastRule)(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, types::TypeRef from, types::TypeRef to);
export typedef void (*LambdaBody)(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *lambda, types::TypeRef type);
// A statement inside a block that is a value and that is not an expression: a local, a `return`, a loop. J1d's walk
// checks it in the scope the block opened. Unset, such a statement is reported as `not yet typed`.
export typedef void (*Statement)(types::Checked *c, const ast::Stmt *s);

export struct Hooks {
    Convert convert;
    CastRule cast;
    LambdaBody lambda_body;
    Statement statement;
};

static Hooks hooks;

export void set_hooks(Hooks h) {
    hooks = h;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The work stack

struct Frame {
    const ast::Expr *node;
    types::TypeRef expected;
    U32 stage;
    // Only for a value statement (stage 100 and up, or its last stage): what the frame is about.
    const ast::Stmt *source;
    Usize index;
    Str message;
    // Only for a `type_value` read as a construction (stage 1): the call it stands for.
    const ast::Expr *twin;
};

struct Work {
    Frame items[:];
    Usize count;
};

static void push_frame(Work *w, Frame frame) {
    if (w->count == countof(w->items)) {
        Usize capacity = countof(w->items) * 2;
        if (capacity < 16) {
            capacity = 16;
        }
        w->items = mem::realloc(w->items, capacity);
    }
    w->items[w->count] = frame;
    w->count += 1;
}

static void push(Work *w, const ast::Expr *node, types::TypeRef expected, U32 stage) {
    if (node == nullptr) {
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef wanted = expected;
    if (wanted != nullptr && wanted->kind == .automatic) {
        wanted = nullptr;
    }
    push_frame(w, (Frame){.node = node, .expected = wanted, .stage = stage});
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Small helpers

// A message built with format::push, as the Str that the pieces of a text take.
static Str msg(char chars[:]) {
    return chars;
}

static Bool same_text(Str a, Str b) {
    if (countof(a) != countof(b)) {
        return false;
    }
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(a); i++) {
        if (a[i] != b[i]) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}

static Bool has_letter(Str s, char lower) {
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(s); i++) {
        if (s[i] == lower || s[i] == lower - 32) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

static Bool is_integer(types::TypeRef t) {
    return t->kind == .integer;
}

static Bool is_number(types::TypeRef t) {
    return t->kind == .integer || t->kind == .floating;
}

static Bool is_scalar(types::TypeRef t) {
    return is_number(t) || t->kind == .bool_type || t->kind == .pointer || t->kind == .enumeration ||
           t->kind == .fn_value;
}

static Bool wants_integer(types::TypeRef t) {
    return t != nullptr && t->kind == .integer;
}

static types::TypeRef invalid(const types::Checked *c) {
    return c->table.invalid;
}

// The recorded type of `e`; the invalid type when there is none.
static types::TypeRef typ(const types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e) {
    types::TypeRef t = types::type_of(c, e);
    if (t == nullptr) {
        return c->table.invalid;
    }
    return t;
}

static void poison(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e) {
    types::set_type(c, e, c->table.invalid);
}

static void not_yet(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, Str what) {
    types::failf(c, e->span, "not yet typed: ", what);
    poison(c, e);
}

static Str path_str(ast::Path path) {
    Usize total = 0;
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(path.segments); i++) {
        if (i > 0) {
            total += 2;
        }
        Str segment = path.segments[i].text;
        total += countof(segment);
    }
    char out[:] = mem::alloc_slice(char, total);
    Usize at = 0;
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(path.segments); i++) {
        if (i > 0) {
            out[at] = ':';
            out[at + 1] = ':';
            at += 2;
        }
        Str segment = path.segments[i].text;
        for (Usize k = 0; k < countof(segment); k++) {
            out[at + k] = segment[k];
        }
        at += countof(segment);
    }
    return msg(out);
}

static U64 integer_value(const ast::Expr *e) {
    if (e->kind == .integer) {
        return e->kind.integer.value;
    }
    return 0;
}

static Bool plain_integer(const ast::Expr *e) {
    return e->kind == .integer && countof(e->kind.integer.suffix) == 0;
}

static Bool plain_float(const ast::Expr *e) {
    return e->kind == .floating && countof(e->kind.floating.suffix) == 0;
}

static Bool negated_literal(const ast::Expr *e) {
    if (!(e->kind == .unary)) {
        return false;
    }
    if (!(e->kind.unary.op == .negate)) {
        return false;
    }
    const ast::Expr *operand = e->kind.unary.operand;
    return plain_integer(operand) || plain_float(operand);
}

// A literal that takes the type of its neighbour: `1`, `2.5`, `-3`.
static Bool literal_like(const ast::Expr *e) {
    return plain_integer(e) || plain_float(e) || negated_literal(e);
}

static Bool is_lvalue(const ast::Expr *e) {
    if (e->kind == .name || e->kind == .member || e->kind == .index) {
        return true;
    }
    if (e->kind == .unary) {
        return e->kind.unary.op == .dereference;
    }
    return false;
}

// The variable a name stands for when it was declared `const`, else null.
static const types::Symbol *const_variable(const types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e) {
    if (!(e->kind == .name)) {
        return nullptr;
    }
    const types::Symbol *s = types::lookup_path(&c->scopes, e->kind.name.path);
    if (s != nullptr && s->kind == .variable && s->is_const) {
        return s;
    }
    return nullptr;
}

// The variable at the root of a place (`x` in `x`, `x.a`, `x[i]`, `(*p).a` is `p`), else null.
static const types::Symbol *root_symbol(const types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e) {
    const ast::Expr *at = e;
    while (at != nullptr) {
        if (at->kind == .name) {
            const types::Symbol *s = types::lookup_path(&c->scopes, at->kind.name.path);
            if (s != nullptr && s->kind == .variable) {
                return s;
            }
            return nullptr;
        }
        if (at->kind == .member) {
            at = at->kind.member.base;
        } else if (at->kind == .index) {
            at = at->kind.index.base;
        } else if (at->kind == .unary && at->kind.unary.op == .dereference) {
            at = at->kind.unary.operand;
        } else {
            return nullptr;
        }
    }
    return nullptr;
}

// A type written inside an expression: `bool` and `char` are types in a body that has smith's own syntax (#1960).
static types::TypeRef resolve_written(types::Checked *c, const ast::TypeExpr *t) {
    c->new_syntax = c->new_body;
    types::TypeRef found = types::resolve(c, t);
    c->new_syntax = false;
    return found;
}

// Whether `e` is written in smith's own syntax: the variable at its root was declared there (#1524).
static Bool in_new_syntax(const types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e) {
    const types::Symbol *s = root_symbol(c, e);
    return s != nullptr && s->is_new;
}

// The constant variable that writing to `e` would change: `x` itself, or `x` in `x.a` and `x[i]` (a field of a
// constant record, an element of a constant array), when `x` is constant and written in smith's own syntax. A write
// through a pointer changes what it points to, not `x`, so a pointer ends the walk. Null when there is none.
static const types::Symbol *constant_place(const types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e) {
    const ast::Expr *at = e;
    while (at != nullptr) {
        if (at->kind == .name) {
            const types::Symbol *s = types::lookup_path(&c->scopes, at->kind.name.path);
            if (s != nullptr && s->kind == .variable && s->is_new && s->is_const) {
                return s;
            }
            return nullptr;
        }
        const ast::Expr *base = nullptr;
        if (at->kind == .member) {
            base = at->kind.member.base;
        } else if (at->kind == .index) {
            base = at->kind.index.base;
        } else {
            return nullptr;
        }
        types::TypeRef t = types::type_of(c, base);
        if (t == nullptr || t->kind == .pointer || t->kind == .slice) {
            return nullptr;
        }
        at = base;
    }
    return nullptr;
}

// The read-only pointer (`*T`, which is `pointee_const`) that writing to `e` goes through, else null: `p.*`, `p.x` and
// `p[i]` where `p` is a `*T`. Only for a place written in smith's own syntax, where `*T` is read-only and `*mut T`
// writable (a C `const T *` keeps the rules it had).
static types::TypeRef readonly_pointer(const types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e) {
    if (!in_new_syntax(c, e)) {
        return nullptr;
    }
    const ast::Expr *at = e;
    while (at != nullptr) {
        const ast::Expr *base = nullptr;
        if (at->kind == .unary && at->kind.unary.op == .dereference) {
            base = at->kind.unary.operand;
        } else if (at->kind == .member) {
            base = at->kind.member.base;
        } else if (at->kind == .index) {
            base = at->kind.index.base;
        } else {
            return nullptr;
        }
        types::TypeRef t = types::type_of(c, base);
        if (t == nullptr) {
            return nullptr;
        }
        if (t->kind == .pointer) {
            if (t->pointee_const) {
                return t;
            }
            return nullptr;
        }
        at = base;
    }
    return nullptr;
}

// The edit that writes `mut ` before the name of `s` (`let x` becomes `let mut x`), when a fix may (a binding written
// in smith's own syntax, with no attribute before it and not `const`), else an empty list.
static syntax::Edits mut_fix(const types::Symbol *s) {
    syntax::Edits edits = {};
    if (s == nullptr || !s->may_mut || !s->is_const) {
        return edits;
    }
    edits = mem::realloc(edits, 1);
    Str word = "mut ";
    edits[0] = (syntax::Edit){.span = {.start = s->at.start, .end = s->at.start}, .text = word};
    return edits;
}

// The edits that let a write go through the pointer `base` (`p` in `p.* = v`): when `p` is written `*T`, its type
// becomes `*mut T`; when `p` is taken from `&x` of a constant `x`, `x` becomes `mut x` in the same fix. Empty when
// neither is so (the pointer comes from somewhere a fix cannot see).
static syntax::Edits pointer_fix(const types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *base) {
    syntax::Edits edits = {};
    if (base->kind != .name) {
        return edits;
    }
    const types::Symbol *p = types::lookup_path(&c->scopes, base->kind.name.path);
    if (p == nullptr || p->kind != .variable || !p->is_new) {
        return edits;
    }
    const ast::TypeExpr *written = p->written;
    if (written != nullptr && written->kind == .pointer && !written->kind.pointer.is_mut) {
        edits = mem::realloc(edits, 1);
        U32 after_star = written->span.start + 1;
        Str word = "mut ";
        edits[0] = (syntax::Edit){.span = {.start = after_star, .end = after_star}, .text = word};
    } else if (written != nullptr && written->kind != .auto_type) {
        return edits;
    }
    const ast::Expr *init = p->init;
    if (init != nullptr && init->kind == .unary && init->kind.unary.op == .address_of &&
        init->kind.unary.operand->kind == .name) {
        const types::Symbol *x = types::lookup_path(&c->scopes, init->kind.unary.operand->kind.name.path);
        syntax::Edits more = mut_fix(x != nullptr && x->kind == .variable ? x : nullptr);
        if (countof(more) == 1) {
            edits = mem::realloc(edits, countof(edits) + 1);
            edits[countof(edits) - 1] = more[0];
        }
    }
    return edits;
}

// The expression whose type is the read-only pointer that writing to `e` goes through (the `base` that
// `readonly_pointer` stops at), else null.
static const ast::Expr *readonly_base(const types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e) {
    const ast::Expr *at = e;
    while (at != nullptr) {
        const ast::Expr *base = nullptr;
        if (at->kind == .unary && at->kind.unary.op == .dereference) {
            base = at->kind.unary.operand;
        } else if (at->kind == .member) {
            base = at->kind.member.base;
        } else if (at->kind == .index) {
            base = at->kind.index.base;
        } else {
            return nullptr;
        }
        types::TypeRef t = types::type_of(c, base);
        if (t == nullptr) {
            return nullptr;
        }
        if (t->kind == .pointer) {
            return base;
        }
        at = base;
    }
    return nullptr;
}

// Whether `e` may be written to; reports and says what to write when it may not.
static Bool writable(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, Str verb) {
    if (!is_lvalue(e)) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "cannot ", verb,
                     " this expression, it is not a place; write a variable, a field, an element or `*pointer`");
        return false;
    }
    const types::Symbol *s = const_variable(c, e);
    if (s != nullptr && !s->is_new) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "cannot ", verb, " `", s->name,
                     "`, it is declared const; remove `const` from its declaration");
        return false;
    }
    const types::Symbol *constant = constant_place(c, e);
    if (constant != nullptr) {
        types::fail_fix(c, e->span,
                        msg(format::push("cannot ", verb, " `", constant->name, "`, `", constant->name,
                                         "` is constant: declare it `mut ", constant->name, "`")),
                        mut_fix(constant));
        return false;
    }
    types::TypeRef through = readonly_pointer(c, e);
    if (through != nullptr) {
        const ast::Expr *base = readonly_base(c, e);
        syntax::Edits fix = base != nullptr ? pointer_fix(c, base) : (syntax::Edits){};
        types::fail_fix(c, e->span,
                        msg(format::push("cannot ", verb, " this place, it is behind the read-only pointer `*",
                                         types::show(through->element), "`; take a `*mut ",
                                         types::show(through->element), "` to write through it")),
                        fix);
        return false;
    }
    return true;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Conformance to the expected type

static Bool is_null_target(types::TypeRef to) {
    return to->kind == .pointer || to->kind == .fn_value;
}

// The default of the convert hook: exact equality, `null` for a pointer, a function for its `Fn`.
static Bool conform(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, types::TypeRef from, types::TypeRef to) {
    if (from->kind == .fn_value && to->kind == .function && from->element == to) {
        return true; // a stored lambda where `fn(A) -> R` is expected (#1649)
    }
    if (hooks.convert != nullptr) {
        return hooks.convert(c, e, from, to);
    }
    if (from == to) {
        return true;
    }
    if (from->kind == .null_type && is_null_target(to)) {
        return true;
    }
    if (from->kind == .function && to->kind == .fn_value && to->element == from) {
        return true;
    }
    Str want = types::show(to);
    types::failf(c, e->span, "expected `", want, "`, found `", types::show(from), "`; write a value of type `", want,
                 "`, or convert it with a conversion such as `value.to(", want, ")`");
    return false;
}

// Records `found` for `e` and checks it against `expected` (null: nothing to check).
static void finish(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, types::TypeRef found, types::TypeRef expected) {
    types::set_type(c, e, found);
    types::TypeRef bad = c->table.invalid;
    if (expected == nullptr || found == bad || expected == bad || found == expected) {
        return;
    }
    if (expected->kind == .automatic) {
        return;
    }
    if (!conform(c, e, found, expected)) {
        poison(c, e);
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Literals

// Whether `value` (negated when `negated`) is a value of the integer type `t`.
static Bool fits(types::TypeRef t, U64 value, Bool negated) {
    U64 one = 1;
    if (t->is_signed) {
        U64 limit = one << (t->bits - 1);
        if (negated) {
            return value <= limit;
        }
        return value < limit;
    }
    if (negated) {
        return value == 0;
    }
    if (t->bits >= 64) {
        return true;
    }
    return value < (one << t->bits);
}

static Str range_text(types::TypeRef t) {
    U64 one = 1;
    if (t->is_signed) {
        U64 limit = one << (t->bits - 1);
        return msg(format::push("-", limit, " to ", limit - 1));
    }
    U64 top = ~(U64)0;
    if (t->bits < 64) {
        top = (one << t->bits) - 1;
    }
    return msg(format::push("0 to ", top));
}

// Checks a literal against the integer type it is given; reports "300 does not fit U8 ...".
static Bool fit(types::Checked *c, ast::Span span, U64 value, Bool negated, types::TypeRef t) {
    if (fits(t, value, negated)) {
        return true;
    }
    Str sign = "";
    if (negated) {
        sign = "-";
    }
    Str range = range_text(t);
    types::failf(c, span, sign, value, " does not fit ", types::show(t), " (", range, "); write a value from ", range,
                 ", or give it a wider type");
    return false;
}

// The type of an integer literal with no expected type: its suffix, else the first of I32, I64, U64 it fits.
static types::TypeRef default_integer(const types::Checked *c, U64 value, Str suffix) {
    Bool unsigned_literal = has_letter(suffix, 'u');
    Bool wide = has_letter(suffix, 'l');
    if (unsigned_literal) {
        if (!wide && value <= 4294967295) {
            return types::builtin(&c->table, "U32");
        }
        return types::builtin(&c->table, "U64");
    }
    if (!wide && value <= 2147483647) {
        return types::builtin(&c->table, "I32");
    }
    if (value <= 9223372036854775807) {
        return types::builtin(&c->table, "I64");
    }
    return types::builtin(&c->table, "U64");
}

static void literal_integer(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .integer)) {
        return;
    }
    U64 value = e->kind.integer.value;
    Str suffix = e->kind.integer.suffix;
    if (countof(suffix) == 0 && wants_integer(f.expected)) {
        if (fit(c, e->span, value, false, f.expected)) {
            types::set_type(c, e, f.expected);
        } else {
            poison(c, e);
        }
        return;
    }
    finish(c, e, default_integer(c, value, suffix), f.expected);
}

// Whether a float literal is in the range of the 16-bit float `t` (`F16` up to 65504, `BF16` up to about 3.39e38);
// reports "70000 does not fit F16 ..." when it is not. `F32` and `F64` take every literal.
static Bool float_fits(types::Checked *c, ast::Span span, F64 value, types::TypeRef t) {
    if (t->bits != 16) {
        return true;
    }
    Bool brain = t->name[0] == 'B';
    F64 top = brain ? 3.3895313892515355e38 : 65504.0;
    if (value <= top) {
        return true;
    }
    Str range = brain ? "up to 3.3895313892515355e38" : "up to 65504";
    types::failf(c, span, "the literal does not fit ", types::show(t), " (", range, "); write a value ", range,
                 ", or give it a wider type");
    return false;
}

static void literal_float(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .floating)) {
        return;
    }
    Str suffix = e->kind.floating.suffix;
    types::TypeRef expected = f.expected;
    if (countof(suffix) == 0 && expected != nullptr && expected->kind == .floating) {
        if (!float_fits(c, e->span, e->kind.floating.value, expected)) {
            poison(c, e);
            return;
        }
        types::set_type(c, e, expected);
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef t = types::builtin(&c->table, "F64");
    if (same_text(suffix, "f") || same_text(suffix, "F")) {
        t = types::builtin(&c->table, "F32");
    }
    finish(c, e, t, expected);
}

static void literal_character(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .character)) {
        return;
    }
    U64 value = e->kind.character.value;
    if (wants_integer(f.expected)) {
        if (fit(c, e->span, value, false, f.expected)) {
            types::set_type(c, e, f.expected);
        } else {
            poison(c, e);
        }
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef t = types::builtin(&c->table, "Char");
    if (value > 255) {
        t = types::builtin(&c->table, "U32");
    }
    finish(c, e, t, f.expected);
}

static void literal_null(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    types::TypeRef expected = f.expected;
    if (expected != nullptr && is_null_target(expected)) {
        types::set_type(c, e, expected);
        return;
    }
    finish(c, e, c->table.null_type, expected);
}

// Retypes an unsuffixed literal (or a negated one) to the number type `to`; false, with the error, when it does not
// fit.
static Bool retype(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *n, types::TypeRef to) {
    if (n->kind == .integer) {
        U64 value = n->kind.integer.value;
        if (!fit(c, n->span, value, false, to)) {
            return false;
        }
        types::set_type(c, n, to);
        return true;
    }
    if (n->kind == .floating) {
        types::set_type(c, n, to);
        return true;
    }
    if (!(n->kind == .unary)) {
        return false;
    }
    const ast::Expr *operand = n->kind.unary.operand;
    if (operand->kind == .integer) {
        U64 value = operand->kind.integer.value;
        if (!fit(c, n->span, value, true, to)) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    types::set_type(c, operand, to);
    types::set_type(c, n, to);
    return true;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Names and variants

static void unknown_name(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, ast::Path path) {
    Str name = path_str(path);
    if (countof(path.segments) > 1) {
        const types::Symbol *first = types::lookup(&c->scopes, path.segments[0].text);
        if (first != nullptr && first->kind == .module_name) {
            not_yet(c, e, msg(format::push("`", name, "`, a name from another module")));
            return;
        }
    }
    if (countof(path.segments) > 1 && same_text(path.segments[0].text, "c")) {
        if (c->options.headers != nullptr) {
            types::failf(c, e->span, "`", name, "` is not declared by any C header this file imports; ",
                         "write `import <h>;` for the header that has it, and check its spelling");
            poison(c, e);
            return;
        }
        not_yet(c, e, msg(format::push("c:: name `", name, "`")));
        return;
    }
    types::failf(c, e->span, "unknown name `", name, "`; declare it before it is used, for example `I32 ", name,
                 " = 0;`, or import the module that has it");
    poison(c, e);
}

// `undefined` written as a bare name: the opt-out of zero, valid only as a local's whole initializer (K3b, #1334).
export Bool is_undefined(const ast::Expr *e) {
    if (e == nullptr || !(e->kind == .name)) return false;
    ast::Path path = e->kind.name.path;
    return countof(path.segments) == 1 && same_text(path.segments[0].text, "undefined");
}

// `m.sin`, `m.b.T`, `m::sin`: names read through modules (#1677). A function or constant takes its type; a module, a
// type and a name that is missing are errors that say so. False when the first name is not a module.
static Bool module_value(types::Checked *c, Frame f, types::Names names) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (names.count < 2 || c->options.load == nullptr) return false;
    const types::Symbol *first = types::scoped(c, names.items[0]);
    if (first == nullptr || first->kind != .module_name) return false;
    types::Through t = types::through_modules(c, &names);
    if (t.symbol == nullptr) {
        types::report_missing(c, e->span, t);
        poison(c, e);
        return true;
    }
    if (t.stopped) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`", t.symbol->name, "` has no members; only a module has names after a `.`");
        poison(c, e);
        return true;
    }
    if (t.symbol->kind == .module_name) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`", types::module_dotted(t.symbol),
                     "` is a module: a module is known only at compile ", "time, so write a name of it, such as `",
                     names.items[names.count - 1], ".name`");
        poison(c, e);
    } else if (t.symbol->kind == .type_name) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`", names.items[names.count - 1], "` is a type, not a value; write a variable, a ",
                     "function or a constant here");
        poison(c, e);
    } else {
        finish(c, e, t.symbol->type, f.expected);
    }
    return true;
}

static void name_expr(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .name)) {
        return;
    }
    ast::Path path = e->kind.name.path;
    const types::Symbol *s = types::lookup_path(&c->scopes, path);
    if (s == nullptr && is_undefined(e)) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`undefined` is only valid as a whole initializer, e.g. `let mut x: i32 = undefined`");
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    if (s == nullptr && countof(path.segments) > 1 && countof(path.segments) <= 8) {
        types::Names names = {.count = countof(path.segments)};
        for (Usize i = 0; i < names.count; i++)
            names.items[i] = path.segments[i].text;
        if (module_value(c, f, names)) return;
    }
    if (s == nullptr) {
        unknown_name(c, e, path);
        return;
    }
    if (s->kind == .module_name) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`", path_str(path), "` is a module: a module is known only at compile time, so it ",
                     "cannot be stored, passed or compared; write a name of it, such as `", path_str(path), ".name`");
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    if (s->kind == .type_name) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`", path_str(path), "` is a type, not a value; write a variable, a function or a ",
                     "constant here");
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    finish(c, e, s->type, f.expected);
}

// Whether an enumerator of a payload enumeration carries a value (`void` is what one that carries nothing has).
static Bool carries_value(types::TypeRef t) {
    if (t->kind == .void_type) {
        return false;
    }
    return true;
}

// What `.name(x)` carries where `expected` is wanted: the `T` of `.some(x)` over `?T`, the type an enumerator of a
// payload enumeration carries; null for anything else (a leaf: `variant_expr` says what is wrong).
static types::TypeRef carried_type(Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    types::TypeRef expected = f.expected;
    if (!(e->kind == .variant) || expected == nullptr || e->kind.variant.payload == nullptr) {
        return nullptr;
    }
    Str name = e->kind.variant.name;
    if (expected->kind == .option) {
        return same_text(name, "some") ? expected->element : nullptr;
    }
    if (expected->kind == .enumeration && expected->has_payload) {
        const types::Slot *slot = types::member(expected, name);
        if (slot != nullptr && carries_value(slot->type)) {
            return slot->type;
        }
    }
    return nullptr;
}

// `.some(x)` or `.circle(x)` where the type is known: not a leaf, its payload is checked as the type it carries.
static Bool is_some_value(Frame f) {
    return carried_type(f) != nullptr;
}

static void some_before(Work *w, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .variant)) {
        return;
    }
    const ast::Expr *payload = e->kind.variant.payload;
    push(w, e, f.expected, 1);
    push(w, payload, carried_type(f), 0);
}

static void some_after(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .variant)) {
        return;
    }
    if (typ(c, e->kind.variant.payload) == invalid(c)) {
        poison(c, e);
    } else {
        types::set_type(c, e, f.expected);
    }
}

static void variant_expr(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .variant)) {
        return;
    }
    Str name = e->kind.variant.name;
    types::TypeRef expected = f.expected;
    if (expected == nullptr) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`.", name,
                     "` needs a type to say what it is; give the variable a type before `=`, or pass it ",
                     "where a parameter has the type");
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    if (expected->kind == .option && same_text(name, "none")) {
        types::set_type(c, e, expected);
        return;
    }
    const types::Slot *slot = nullptr;
    if (expected->kind == .enumeration) {
        slot = types::member(expected, name);
    }
    if (slot != nullptr && expected->has_payload && carries_value(slot->type)) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`.", name, "` carries a `", types::show(slot->type), "`; write `.", name, "(value)`");
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    if (slot != nullptr && e->kind.variant.payload != nullptr) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`.", name, "` carries no value; write `.", name, "` alone");
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    if (slot != nullptr) {
        types::set_type(c, e, expected);
        return;
    }
    Str want = types::show(expected);
    if (expected->kind == .enumeration) {
        Str first = "its enumerators";
        if (countof(expected->slots) > 0) {
            first = expected->slots[0].name;
        }
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`", want, "` has no enumerator `", name,
                     "`; write one of its enumerators, such as `.", first, "`");
    } else {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`.", name, "` is not a value of `", want, "`; write a value of type `", want, "`");
    }
    poison(c, e);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Unary operators

static Str unary_text(ast::UnaryOp op) {
    if (op == .negate) {
        return "-";
    }
    if (op == .plus) {
        return "+";
    }
    if (op == .logical_not) {
        return "!";
    }
    if (op == .bit_not) {
        return "~";
    }
    if (op == .address_of) {
        return "&";
    }
    if (op == .dereference) {
        return "*";
    }
    if (op == .pre_increment || op == .post_increment) {
        return "++";
    }
    return "--";
}

// A unary operator that wants a number, an integer or a Bool; reports what to write when `t` is none of them.
static types::TypeRef unary_value(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, ast::UnaryOp op, types::TypeRef t) {
    Str text = unary_text(op);
    Str found = types::show(t);
    if (op == .logical_not) {
        if (t->kind == .bool_type) {
            return t;
        }
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`!` needs a Bool, found `", found, "`; write a comparison such as `x == 0`");
    } else if (op == .bit_not) {
        if (is_integer(t)) {
            return t;
        }
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`~` needs an integer, found `", found, "`; write an integer such as `U32` or `I64`");
    } else {
        if (is_number(t)) {
            return t;
        }
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`", text, "` needs a number, found `", found,
                     "`; write an integer or floating value");
    }
    return invalid(c);
}

static types::TypeRef unary_address(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, const ast::Expr *operand, types::TypeRef t) {
    if (!is_lvalue(operand)) {
        types::failf(c, e->span,
                     "cannot take the address of a value that is not a place; store it in a variable first and write "
                     "`&variable`");
        return invalid(c);
    }
    // `&x` is `*mut T` when `x` is `mut`, else `*T` (the same for `&x.a` and `&xs[i]`; through a `*T`, read-only).
    Bool constant = const_variable(c, operand) != nullptr || constant_place(c, operand) != nullptr ||
                    readonly_pointer(c, operand) != nullptr;
    return types::pointer_to(&c->table, t, constant);
}

static types::TypeRef unary_dereference(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, types::TypeRef t) {
    if (t->kind != .pointer) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`*` needs a pointer, found `", types::show(t), "`; write a pointer such as `",
                     types::show(t), " *`");
        return invalid(c);
    }
    if (t->element->kind == .void_type) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "cannot dereference `", types::show(t),
                     "`; cast it to a typed pointer first, as in `(U8 *)p`");
        return invalid(c);
    }
    return t->element;
}

static types::TypeRef unary_step(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, const ast::Expr *operand, ast::UnaryOp op,
                                 types::TypeRef t) {
    if (!writable(c, operand, "change")) {
        return invalid(c);
    }
    if (is_integer(t) || t->kind == .pointer) {
        return t;
    }
    types::failf(c, e->span, "`", unary_text(op), "` needs an integer or a pointer, found `", types::show(t),
                 "`; write `x += 1.0` for a floating value");
    return invalid(c);
}

static types::TypeRef unary_type(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, ast::UnaryOp op, const ast::Expr *operand,
                                 types::TypeRef t) {
    if (op == .address_of) {
        return unary_address(c, e, operand, t);
    }
    if (op == .dereference) {
        return unary_dereference(c, e, t);
    }
    if (op == .negate || op == .plus || op == .logical_not || op == .bit_not) {
        return unary_value(c, e, op, t);
    }
    return unary_step(c, e, operand, op, t);
}

static void unary_before(types::Checked *c, Work *w, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .unary)) {
        return;
    }
    ast::UnaryOp op = e->kind.unary.op;
    const ast::Expr *operand = e->kind.unary.operand;
    if (op == .negate && wants_integer(f.expected) && operand->kind == .integer) {
        U64 value = operand->kind.integer.value;
        if (fit(c, e->span, value, true, f.expected)) {
            types::set_type(c, operand, f.expected);
            types::set_type(c, e, f.expected);
        } else {
            poison(c, operand);
            poison(c, e);
        }
        return;
    }
    push(w, e, f.expected, 1);
    push(w, operand, nullptr, 0);
}

static void unary_after(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .unary)) {
        return;
    }
    ast::UnaryOp op = e->kind.unary.op;
    const ast::Expr *operand = e->kind.unary.operand;
    types::TypeRef t = typ(c, operand);
    if (t == invalid(c)) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef result = unary_type(c, e, op, operand, t);
    if (result == invalid(c)) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    finish(c, e, result, f.expected);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Binary operators

static Str binary_text(ast::BinaryOp op) {
    if (op == .add) {
        return "+";
    }
    if (op == .subtract) {
        return "-";
    }
    if (op == .multiply) {
        return "*";
    }
    if (op == .divide) {
        return "/";
    }
    if (op == .remainder) {
        return "%";
    }
    if (op == .shift_left) {
        return "<<";
    }
    if (op == .shift_right) {
        return ">>";
    }
    if (op == .less) {
        return "<";
    }
    if (op == .greater) {
        return ">";
    }
    if (op == .less_equal) {
        return "<=";
    }
    if (op == .greater_equal) {
        return ">=";
    }
    if (op == .equal) {
        return "==";
    }
    if (op == .not_equal) {
        return "!=";
    }
    if (op == .bit_and) {
        return "&";
    }
    if (op == .bit_xor) {
        return "^";
    }
    if (op == .bit_or) {
        return "|";
    }
    if (op == .logical_and) {
        return "&&";
    }
    return "||";
}

static Bool is_shift(ast::BinaryOp op) {
    return op == .shift_left || op == .shift_right;
}

static Bool is_logical(ast::BinaryOp op) {
    return op == .logical_and || op == .logical_or;
}

static Bool is_ordering(ast::BinaryOp op) {
    return op == .less || op == .greater || op == .less_equal || op == .greater_equal;
}

static Bool is_equality(ast::BinaryOp op) {
    return op == .equal || op == .not_equal;
}

static Bool is_bitwise(ast::BinaryOp op) {
    return op == .bit_and || op == .bit_xor || op == .bit_or;
}

// Both operands the same type; reports which cast to write when they differ.
static Bool same_operands(types::Checked *c, ast::Span span, ast::BinaryOp op, types::TypeRef l, types::TypeRef r) {
    if (l == r) {
        return true;
    }
    Str left = types::show(l);
    types::failf(c, span, "the operands of `", binary_text(op), "` have different types, `", left, "` and `",
                 types::show(r), "`; make both `", left, "` with a conversion, for example `y.to(", left, ")`");
    return false;
}

static types::TypeRef need_number(types::Checked *c, ast::Span span, ast::BinaryOp op, types::TypeRef t,
                                  Bool integer_only) {
    Str what = "a number";
    if (integer_only) {
        what = "integers";
    }
    types::failf(c, span, "`", binary_text(op), "` needs ", what, ", found `", types::show(t), "`; write ",
                 integer_only ? "an integer such as `U32` or `I64`" : "an integer or floating value");
    return invalid(c);
}

static types::TypeRef pointer_arithmetic(types::Checked *c, ast::Span span, ast::BinaryOp op, types::TypeRef l,
                                         types::TypeRef r) {
    if (l->kind == .pointer && is_integer(r)) {
        return l;
    }
    if (op == .add && is_integer(l) && r->kind == .pointer) {
        return r;
    }
    if (op == .subtract && l->kind == .pointer && l == r) {
        return types::builtin(&c->table, "Isize");
    }
    types::failf(c, span, "cannot ", op == .add ? "add" : "subtract", " `", types::show(r), "` ",
                 op == .add ? "to" : "from", " `", types::show(l), "`; write a pointer and an integer, as in `p + 1`");
    return invalid(c);
}

static types::TypeRef arith_type(types::Checked *c, ast::Span span, ast::BinaryOp op, types::TypeRef l,
                                 types::TypeRef r) {
    if ((op == .add || op == .subtract) && (l->kind == .pointer || r->kind == .pointer)) {
        return pointer_arithmetic(c, span, op, l, r);
    }
    if (!is_number(l)) {
        return need_number(c, span, op, l, false);
    }
    if (!same_operands(c, span, op, l, r)) {
        return invalid(c);
    }
    if (op == .remainder && !is_integer(l)) {
        return need_number(c, span, op, l, true);
    }
    return l;
}

static types::TypeRef shift_type(types::Checked *c, ast::Span span, ast::BinaryOp op, types::TypeRef l,
                                 types::TypeRef r) {
    if (!is_integer(l)) {
        return need_number(c, span, op, l, true);
    }
    if (!is_integer(r)) {
        return need_number(c, span, op, r, true);
    }
    return l;
}

static types::TypeRef bitwise_type(types::Checked *c, ast::Span span, ast::BinaryOp op, types::TypeRef l,
                                   types::TypeRef r) {
    if (!is_integer(l) && l->kind != .bool_type) {
        return need_number(c, span, op, l, true);
    }
    if (!same_operands(c, span, op, l, r)) {
        return invalid(c);
    }
    return l;
}

static types::TypeRef logical_type(types::Checked *c, ast::Span span, ast::BinaryOp op, types::TypeRef l,
                                   types::TypeRef r) {
    types::TypeRef bad = l;
    if (l->kind == .bool_type) {
        bad = r;
    }
    if (bad->kind == .bool_type) {
        return c->table.bool_type;
    }
    types::failf(c, span, "`", binary_text(op), "` needs Bool operands, found `", types::show(bad),
                 "`; write a comparison such as `x != 0`");
    return invalid(c);
}

static Bool null_against(types::TypeRef a, types::TypeRef b) {
    return a->kind == .null_type && is_null_target(b);
}

// The first element of the tuple `t` (nested tuples included) that `==` cannot compare, or null. A walk with an
// explicit stack, never recursion.
static types::TypeRef uncomparable_element(types::TypeRef t) {
    Usize capacity = 8;
    types::Slot pending[:] = mem::alloc_slice(types::Slot, capacity);
    Usize top = 1;
    pending[0].type = t;
    while (top > 0) {
        top -= 1;
        types::TypeRef x = pending[top].type;
        if (x->kind != .tuple) {
            if (!is_scalar(x)) return x;
            continue;
        }
        while (top + countof(x->slots) > capacity) {
            capacity *= 2;
            pending = mem::realloc(pending, capacity);
        }
        for (Usize i = countof(x->slots); i > 0; i--) {
            pending[top].type = x->slots[i - 1].type;
            top += 1;
        }
    }
    return nullptr;
}

static types::TypeRef tuple_compare(types::Checked *c, ast::Span span, ast::BinaryOp op, types::TypeRef l,
                                    types::TypeRef r) {
    if (!is_equality(op)) {
        types::failf(c, span, "`", binary_text(op), "` cannot order `", types::show(l),
                     "`; a tuple is compared with `==` and `!=`, or compare its elements one by one");
        return invalid(c);
    }
    types::TypeRef bad = uncomparable_element(l);
    if (bad != nullptr) {
        types::failf(c, span, "`", binary_text(op), "` cannot compare `", types::show(l), "`: its element `",
                     types::show(bad), "` has no `==`; compare the elements that have one");
        return invalid(c);
    }
    if (!same_operands(c, span, op, l, r)) {
        return invalid(c);
    }
    return c->table.bool_type;
}

static types::TypeRef compare_type(types::Checked *c, ast::Span span, ast::BinaryOp op, types::TypeRef l,
                                   types::TypeRef r) {
    if (is_equality(op) && (null_against(l, r) || null_against(r, l))) {
        return c->table.bool_type;
    }
    if (is_equality(op) && l->kind == .text && r->kind == .text) {
        types::failf(c, span, "`", binary_text(op),
                     "` does not compare two `Str`; compare their bytes with `mem::equal(a, b)`");
        return invalid(c);
    }
    if (l->kind == .tuple && r->kind == .tuple) {
        return tuple_compare(c, span, op, l, r);
    }
    if (!is_scalar(l)) {
        types::failf(c, span, "`", binary_text(op), "` cannot compare `", types::show(l),
                     "`; write numbers, Bools, enumerators or pointers");
        return invalid(c);
    }
    if (is_ordering(op) && l->kind != .pointer && !is_number(l)) {
        return need_number(c, span, op, l, false);
    }
    if (!same_operands(c, span, op, l, r)) {
        return invalid(c);
    }
    return c->table.bool_type;
}

static types::TypeRef binary_type(types::Checked *c, ast::Span span, ast::BinaryOp op, types::TypeRef l,
                                  types::TypeRef r) {
    if (is_logical(op)) {
        return logical_type(c, span, op, l, r);
    }
    if (is_shift(op)) {
        return shift_type(c, span, op, l, r);
    }
    if (is_ordering(op) || is_equality(op)) {
        return compare_type(c, span, op, l, r);
    }
    if (is_bitwise(op)) {
        return bitwise_type(c, span, op, l, r);
    }
    return arith_type(c, span, op, l, r);
}

// Whether a literal of type `from` can take the type `to` of its neighbour.
static Bool adaptable(types::TypeRef from, types::TypeRef to) {
    if (from == to) {
        return false;
    }
    return (is_integer(from) && is_integer(to)) || (from->kind == .floating && to->kind == .floating);
}

// A literal next to a typed operand takes its type; two literals take the expected type of the whole.
static Bool adapt_literals(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *left, const ast::Expr *right, types::TypeRef expected) {
    types::TypeRef lt = typ(c, left);
    types::TypeRef rt = typ(c, right);
    Bool left_literal = literal_like(left);
    Bool right_literal = literal_like(right);
    if (left_literal && !right_literal && adaptable(lt, rt)) {
        return retype(c, left, rt);
    }
    if (right_literal && !left_literal && adaptable(rt, lt)) {
        return retype(c, right, lt);
    }
    if (left_literal && right_literal && expected != nullptr && is_number(expected)) {
        Bool ok = true;
        if (adaptable(lt, expected)) {
            ok = retype(c, left, expected);
        }
        if (ok && adaptable(rt, expected)) {
            ok = retype(c, right, expected);
        }
        return ok;
    }
    return true;
}

// The vector type of `l` or `r`: the one that is a vector, or null when neither is.
static types::TypeRef vector_side(types::TypeRef l, types::TypeRef r) {
    if (l->kind == .vector) {
        return l;
    }
    if (r->kind == .vector) {
        return r;
    }
    return nullptr;
}

// A binary operator with a vector: both sides the same vector, or a vector and a scalar of its element (broadcast).
static void vector_binary(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, types::TypeRef v) {
    if (!(e->kind == .binary)) {
        return;
    }
    ast::BinaryOp op = e->kind.binary.op;
    const ast::Expr *left = e->kind.binary.left;
    const ast::Expr *right = e->kind.binary.right;
    types::TypeRef element = v->element;
    if (typ(c, left)->kind != .vector && literal_like(left) && is_number(typ(c, left)) && !retype(c, left, element)) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    if (typ(c, right)->kind != .vector && literal_like(right) && is_number(typ(c, right)) &&
        !retype(c, right, element)) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef l = typ(c, left);
    types::TypeRef r = typ(c, right);
    Bool same = (l == v || l == element) && (r == v || r == element);
    if (!same) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "the operands of `", binary_text(op), "` are `", types::show(l), "` and `",
                     types::show(r), "`; write two `", types::show(v), "`, or a `", types::show(v), "` and a `",
                     types::show(element), "`");
        poison(c, e);
    } else if (is_logical(op) || is_shift(op) || is_bitwise(op)) {
        not_yet(c, e, msg(format::push("`", binary_text(op), "` on `", types::show(v), "`")));
    } else if (op == .remainder && !is_integer(element)) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`%` needs integers, found `", types::show(v), "`; write a vector of integers");
        poison(c, e);
    } else if (is_ordering(op) || is_equality(op)) {
        finish(c, e, types::vector_of(&c->table, c->table.bool_type, v->count), nullptr);
    } else {
        finish(c, e, v, nullptr);
    }
}

static void binary_after(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .binary)) {
        return;
    }
    ast::BinaryOp op = e->kind.binary.op;
    const ast::Expr *left = e->kind.binary.left;
    const ast::Expr *right = e->kind.binary.right;
    types::TypeRef bad = invalid(c);
    if (typ(c, left) == bad || typ(c, right) == bad) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef vector = vector_side(typ(c, left), typ(c, right));
    if (vector != nullptr) {
        vector_binary(c, e, vector);
        if (typ(c, e) != bad) {
            finish(c, e, typ(c, e), f.expected);
        }
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef expected = nullptr;
    if (!is_ordering(op) && !is_equality(op)) {
        expected = f.expected;
    }
    if (!is_shift(op) && !is_logical(op) && !adapt_literals(c, left, right, expected)) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef t = binary_type(c, e->span, op, typ(c, left), typ(c, right));
    if (t == bad) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    finish(c, e, t, f.expected);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Assignment and the conditional

static ast::BinaryOp binary_of(ast::AssignOp op) {
    if (op == .add) {
        return .add;
    }
    if (op == .subtract) {
        return .subtract;
    }
    if (op == .multiply) {
        return .multiply;
    }
    if (op == .divide) {
        return .divide;
    }
    if (op == .remainder) {
        return .remainder;
    }
    if (op == .shift_left) {
        return .shift_left;
    }
    if (op == .shift_right) {
        return .shift_right;
    }
    if (op == .bit_and) {
        return .bit_and;
    }
    if (op == .bit_xor) {
        return .bit_xor;
    }
    return .bit_or;
}

// Stage 1: the target is typed; the value is wanted as the target's type.
static void assign_target_done(types::Checked *c, Work *w, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .assign)) {
        return;
    }
    ast::AssignOp op = e->kind.assign.op;
    const ast::Expr *target = e->kind.assign.target;
    const ast::Expr *value = e->kind.assign.value;
    types::TypeRef tt = typ(c, target);
    if (target->kind == .index && typ(c, target->kind.index.base)->kind == .vector &&
        typ(c, target->kind.index.base)->element->kind == .bool_type) {
        types::failf(c, target->span, "a lane of `", types::show(typ(c, target->kind.index.base)),
                     "` cannot be assigned; it is the result of a comparison, so read it or build a new vector");
        tt = invalid(c);
    }
    if (tt == invalid(c) || !writable(c, target, "assign to")) {
        push(w, e, nullptr, 3);
        push(w, value, nullptr, 0);
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef hint = nullptr;
    if (op == .plain || (literal_like(value) && op != .shift_left && op != .shift_right)) {
        hint = tt;
    }
    push(w, e, f.expected, 2);
    push(w, value, hint, 0);
}

// Stage 2: a compound assignment `x op= v` is `x = x op v` for the operand rules.
static void assign_value_done(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .assign)) {
        return;
    }
    ast::AssignOp op = e->kind.assign.op;
    types::TypeRef tt = typ(c, e->kind.assign.target);
    types::TypeRef vt = typ(c, e->kind.assign.value);
    if (vt == invalid(c)) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    if (op != .plain) {
        ast::BinaryOp as_binary = binary_of(op);
        if (binary_type(c, e->span, as_binary, tt, vt) == invalid(c)) {
            poison(c, e);
            return;
        }
    }
    finish(c, e, tt, f.expected);
}

static void conditional_before(Work *w, types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .conditional)) {
        return;
    }
    const ast::Expr *first = e->kind.conditional.then_value;
    const ast::Expr *condition = e->kind.conditional.condition;
    push(w, e, f.expected, 1);
    push(w, first, f.expected, 0);
    push(w, condition, c->table.bool_type, 0);
}

// Stage 1: the condition and the first branch are typed; the other is wanted as the first's type.
static void conditional_branch(Work *w, types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .conditional)) {
        return;
    }
    const ast::Expr *second = e->kind.conditional.else_value;
    types::TypeRef first = typ(c, e->kind.conditional.then_value);
    types::TypeRef want = f.expected;
    if (want == nullptr && first != invalid(c)) {
        want = first;
    }
    push(w, e, f.expected, 2);
    push(w, second, want, 0);
}

static void conditional_after(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .conditional)) {
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef first = typ(c, e->kind.conditional.then_value);
    types::TypeRef second = typ(c, e->kind.conditional.else_value);
    types::TypeRef condition = typ(c, e->kind.conditional.condition);
    types::TypeRef bad = invalid(c);
    if (first == bad || second == bad || condition == bad) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    types::set_type(c, e, first);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Calls

// The function type a value of type `t` is called through: a function, an `Fn(F)` value, a pointer to a function.
static types::TypeRef callable(types::TypeRef t) {
    if (t->kind == .function) {
        return t;
    }
    if (t->kind == .fn_value) {
        return t->element;
    }
    if (t->kind == .pointer && t->element->kind == .function) {
        return t->element;
    }
    return nullptr;
}

static Bool is_named(const ast::Expr *arg) {
    return arg->kind == .named_arg;
}

static Bool has_named(ast::ExprRef args[:]) {
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(args); i++) {
        if (is_named(args[i])) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

// The expression an argument gives: the value of `name = value`, else the argument itself.
static const ast::Expr *argument_value(const ast::Expr *arg) {
    if (arg->kind == .named_arg) {
        return arg->kind.named_arg.value;
    }
    return arg;
}

static void push_arguments(Work *w, ast::ExprRef args[:], types::TypeRef signature) {
    for (Usize i = countof(args); i > 0; i--) {
        types::TypeRef wanted = nullptr;
        if (signature != nullptr && i - 1 < countof(signature->slots)) {
            wanted = signature->slots[i - 1].type;
        }
        push(w, argument_value(args[i - 1]), wanted, 0);
    }
}

// A `name = value` argument has the type of its value.
static void type_named_arguments(types::Checked *c, ast::ExprRef args[:]) {
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(args); i++) {
        const ast::Expr *arg = args[i];
        if (arg->kind == .named_arg) {
            types::set_type(c, arg, typ(c, arg->kind.named_arg.value));
        }
    }
}

// How many arguments a call must give: every parameter up to the last one without a default.
static Usize required_arguments(ast::Param params[:], Usize wanted) {
    if (countof(params) != wanted) return wanted;
    Usize required = 0;
    Usize keywords = 0;
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(params); i++) {
        if (params[i].default_value != nullptr) continue;
        if (params[i].is_keyword) {
            keywords += 1;
        } else {
            required = i + 1;
        }
    }
    return required + keywords;
}

// Reports a wrong argument count; true when the count is right. A parameter with a default may be left out.
static Bool arity_ok(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, types::TypeRef signature, ast::Param params[:],
                     Usize given) {
    Usize wanted = countof(signature->slots);
    Usize required = required_arguments(params, wanted);
    if ((given >= required && given <= wanted) || (given > wanted && signature->variadic)) {
        return true;
    }
    Str plural = "s";
    if (wanted == 1) {
        plural = "";
    }
    if (required != wanted) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "this call passes ", given, " argument", given == 1 ? "" : "s",
                     " but the function takes ", required, " to ", wanted, " arguments, `", types::show(signature),
                     "`");
        return false;
    }
    types::failf(c, e->span, "this call passes ", given, " argument", given == 1 ? "" : "s", " but the function takes ",
                 wanted, " argument", plural, ", `", types::show(signature), "`; write exactly ", wanted, " argument",
                 plural);
    return false;
}

// The declaration of the function a call names, when the callee is a plain name (its parameters have names).
static const ast::Decl *callee_function(const types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *callee) {
    if (callee->kind != .name) {
        return nullptr;
    }
    const types::Symbol *s = types::lookup_path(&c->scopes, callee->kind.name.path);
    if (s != nullptr && s->kind == .function_symbol && s->decl != nullptr && s->decl->kind == .function) {
        return s->decl;
    }
    return nullptr;
}

static Str parameter_list(ast::Param params[:]) {
    if (countof(params) == 0) {
        return "none";
    }
    return msg(format::push("`", params[0].name, "`"));
}

// Which parameter does the argument `i` of a call with names fill: by position, or by its name. Reports an unknown
// name, a name given twice and a name that a position already filled; returns countof(params) for those.
static Usize argument_slot(types::Checked *c, ast::ExprRef args[:], Usize i, ast::Param params[:], Bool taken[:]) {
    Usize slot = i;
    const ast::Expr *arg = args[i];
    if (arg->kind == .named_arg) {
        Str name = arg->kind.named_arg.name;
        slot = countof(params);
        for (Usize k = 0; k < countof(params); k++) {
            if (same_text(params[k].name, name)) {
                slot = k;
            }
        }
        if (slot == countof(params)) {
            types::failf(c, args[i]->span, "the function has no parameter `", name,
                         "`; name one of its parameters, such as ", parameter_list(params));
            return slot;
        }
    }
    if (slot < countof(taken) && taken[slot]) {
        types::failf(c, args[i]->span, "the parameter `", params[slot].name,
                     "` is given twice; give each parameter once");
        return countof(params);
    }
    if (slot < countof(taken)) {
        taken[slot] = true;
    }
    return slot;
}

// Pushes the arguments of a call that names some, each against the type of the parameter it fills.
static void push_named_arguments(types::Checked *c, Work *w, ast::ExprRef args[:], types::TypeRef signature,
                                 ast::Param params[:]) {
    Bool taken[:] = mem::alloc_slice(Bool, countof(params));
    Usize slots[:] = mem::alloc_slice(Usize, countof(args));
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(args); i++) {
        slots[i] = argument_slot(c, args, i, params, taken);
    }
    for (Usize i = countof(args); i > 0; i--) {
        types::TypeRef wanted = nullptr;
        if (slots[i - 1] < countof(signature->slots)) {
            wanted = signature->slots[slots[i - 1]].type;
        }
        push(w, argument_value(args[i - 1]), wanted, 0);
    }
}

// Whether some argument of the call is written `name = ...`.
static Bool names_argument(ast::ExprRef args[:], Str name) {
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(args); i++) {
        const ast::Expr *arg = args[i];
        if (arg->kind == .named_arg) {
            if (same_text(arg->kind.named_arg.name, name)) return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

// A positional argument for a keyword-only parameter (one after the `;` of the declaration) is an error, and so is
// leaving out a keyword-only parameter that has no default.
static void check_keyword_only(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, ast::ExprRef args[:], ast::Param params[:]) {
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(args) && i < countof(params); i++) {
        if (!is_named(args[i]) && params[i].is_keyword) {
            types::failf(c, args[i]->span, "`", params[i].name, "` is keyword-only; write `", params[i].name,
                         " = ...`");
        }
    }
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(params); i++) {
        const ast::Param *param = &params[i];
        if (param->is_keyword && param->default_value == nullptr && !names_argument(args, param->name)) {
            types::failf(c, e->span, "`", param->name, "` is keyword-only and has no default; write `", param->name,
                         " = ...` in the call");
        }
    }
}

static void call_callee_done(types::Checked *c, Work *w, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .call)) {
        return;
    }
    ast::ExprRef args[:] = e->kind.call.args;
    const ast::Expr *callee = e->kind.call.callee;
    types::TypeRef ct = typ(c, callee);
    types::TypeRef signature = callable(ct);
    types_fn::note_arguments(args);
    push(w, e, f.expected, 2);
    if (ct == invalid(c)) {
        push_arguments(w, args, nullptr);
        return;
    }
    if (signature == nullptr) {
        types::failf(c, callee->span, "cannot call a value of type `", types::show(ct),
                     "`, it is not a function; call a function or an `Fn(...)` value");
        push_arguments(w, args, nullptr);
        return;
    }
    const ast::Decl *function = callee_function(c, callee);
    ast::Param params[:] = {};
    if (function != nullptr && function->kind == .function) {
        params = function->kind.function.params;
    }
    if (!arity_ok(c, e, signature, params, countof(args))) {
        signature = nullptr;
    }
    if (signature != nullptr) check_keyword_only(c, e, args, params);
    if (has_named(args) && signature != nullptr) {
        if (countof(params) != countof(signature->slots)) {
            types::failf(c, e->span,
                         "named arguments need a function whose parameters have names; call a function "
                         "by its name, or pass the arguments by position");
            push_arguments(w, args, nullptr);
            return;
        }
        push_named_arguments(c, w, args, signature, params);
        return;
    }
    push_arguments(w, args, signature);
}

static void call_after(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .call)) {
        return;
    }
    type_named_arguments(c, e->kind.call.args);
    types::TypeRef signature = callable(typ(c, e->kind.call.callee));
    if (signature == nullptr) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    finish(c, e, signature->element, f.expected);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Method calls: `x.f(args)` is `f(x, args)` or `f(&x, args)` (#1793, #1795 of #1428)

// Whether a call's callee `x.f` may be a method: a `.member` whose chain of names does not start at a module (that is
// `m.f`, read through the module).
static Bool method_shaped(const types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *callee) {
    if (callee->kind != .member || callee->kind.member.arrow) {
        return false;
    }
    types::Names names = types::member_names(callee);
    if (names.count >= 2 && c->options.load != nullptr) {
        const types::Symbol *first = types::scoped(c, names.items[0]);
        if (first != nullptr && first->kind == .module_name) {
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}

// `lib::list::` of the type `lib::list::List`: the prefix of the names of its module; empty for the current module.
static Str module_prefix(Str type_name) {
    for (Usize i = countof(type_name); i >= 2; i--) {
        if (type_name[i - 1] == ':' && type_name[i - 2] == ':') {
            return type_name[0:i];
        }
    }
    return "";
}

static const types::Symbol *function_named(const types::Checked *c, Str name, Bool public_only) {
    const types::Symbol *s = types::lookup(&c->scopes, name);
    if (s == nullptr || s->kind != .function_symbol || s->type == nullptr || s->type->kind != .function) {
        return nullptr;
    }
    if (public_only && !types::is_public(s)) {
        return nullptr;
    }
    return s;
}

// The type a function's first parameter takes, seen through one pointer.
static types::TypeRef first_parameter(const types::Symbol *s) {
    if (countof(s->type->slots) == 0) {
        return nullptr;
    }
    types::TypeRef t = s->type->slots[0].type;
    if (t->kind == .pointer) {
        return t->element;
    }
    return t;
}

// The call `f(x, args)`; `f(&x, args)` when `f` takes a pointer and `x` is not one, `f(x.*, args)` for the reverse.
static const ast::Expr *method_call(const ast::Expr *e, const types::Symbol *fn, types::TypeRef base_type) {
    if (!(e->kind == .call)) {
        return e;
    }
    const ast::Expr *callee = e->kind.call.callee;
    if (!(callee->kind == .member)) {
        return e;
    }
    ast::ExprRef given[:] = e->kind.call.args;
    // Not mem::alloc_slice: its instance for a pointer element draws the lint of a multilevel conversion.
    ast::ExprRef *block =
        (ast::ExprRef *)mem::alloc_array_raw(sizeof(ast::ExprRef), countof(given) + 1, alignof(ast::ExprRef));
    ast::ExprRef all[:] = block[0:countof(given) + 1];
    ast::Expr *self = (ast::Expr *)callee->kind.member.base;
    types::TypeRef takes = fn->type->slots[0].type;
    if (takes->kind == .pointer && base_type->kind != .pointer) {
        ast::UnaryOp op = .address_of;
        ast::ExprKind address = {.unary = {.op = op, .operand = self}};
        self = ast::expr(address);
        self->span = callee->kind.member.base->span;
    } else if (takes->kind != .pointer && base_type->kind == .pointer) {
        self = ast::deref(self);
        self->span = callee->kind.member.base->span;
    }
    all[0] = self;
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(given); i++) {
        all[i + 1] = given[i];
    }
    ast::Expr *name = ast::name(fn->name);
    name->span = callee->span;
    ast::Expr *made = ast::call(name, all);
    made->span = e->span;
    return made;
}

// Stage 7: the base of `x.f(args)` is typed. A field `f` of a record is called as a value; else `f` is a function of
// the module that defines the type of `x` or of this module, and the call is the one written with `x` first.
// Reports a method call whose function is missing or does not take the receiver first.
static void method_missing(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, const types::Symbol *fn, types::TypeRef bt) {
    if (!(e->kind == .call)) {
        return;
    }
    const ast::Expr *callee = e->kind.call.callee;
    if (!(callee->kind == .member)) {
        return;
    }
    Str name = callee->kind.member.name;
    Str shown = types::show(bt);
    if (fn == nullptr) {
        types::failf(c, callee->span, "`", shown, "` has no method `", name, "`; write a function `", name,
                     "` whose first parameter is a `", shown, "`, in this module or in the module that defines `",
                     shown, "`");
    } else {
        types::failf(c, callee->span, "the function `", name, "` does not take a `", shown,
                     "` first, so it is not a method of it; write `", name, "(...)` with the right arguments");
    }
    poison(c, e);
}

static void method_resolved(types::Checked *c, Work *w, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .call)) {
        return;
    }
    const ast::Expr *callee = e->kind.call.callee;
    if (!(callee->kind == .member)) {
        return;
    }
    ast::ExprRef args[:] = e->kind.call.args;
    const ast::Expr *base = callee->kind.member.base;
    Str name = callee->kind.member.name;
    types::TypeRef bt = typ(c, base);
    if (bt == invalid(c)) {
        poison(c, e);
        push_arguments(w, args, nullptr);
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef record = bt;
    if (bt->kind == .pointer && bt->element->kind == .record) {
        record = bt->element;
    }
    Str prefix = "";
    if (record->kind == .record) {
        prefix = module_prefix(record->name);
    }
    const types::Symbol *own = nullptr;
    if (countof(prefix) > 0) {
        own = function_named(c, msg(format::push(prefix, name)), true);
    }
    const types::Symbol *local = function_named(c, name, false);
    if (record->kind == .record && types::member(record, name) != nullptr) {
        if (own != nullptr || local != nullptr) {
            types::failf(c, e->span, "`", types::show(record), "` has a field `", name, "` and a function `", name,
                         "`; rename one, a field and a method of one type cannot share a name");
            poison(c, e);
            push_arguments(w, args, nullptr);
            return;
        }
        push(w, e, f.expected, 1);
        push(w, callee, nullptr, 0);
        return;
    }
    const types::Symbol *fn = own != nullptr ? own : local;
    types::TypeRef self_type = bt->kind == .pointer ? bt->element : bt;
    if (own != nullptr && local != nullptr && local != own && first_parameter(own) != nullptr &&
        first_parameter(own) == first_parameter(local)) {
        ast::Span at = local->decl != nullptr ? local->decl->span : e->span;
        types::failf(c, at, "`", name, "` takes a `", types::show(first_parameter(local)), "` as `", prefix, name,
                     "` does, so `x.", name, "()` would be ambiguous; rename this function");
        poison(c, e);
        push_arguments(w, args, nullptr);
        return;
    }
    if (fn == nullptr || first_parameter(fn) == nullptr || first_parameter(fn) != self_type) {
        method_missing(c, e, fn, bt);
        push_arguments(w, args, nullptr);
        return;
    }
    const ast::Expr *made = method_call(e, fn, bt);
    types::set_method(c, e, made);
    push(w, e, nullptr, 8);
    push(w, made, f.expected, 0);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Streams: `stream::next(s)` and `stream::close(s)` on a local `stream::Stream(T)` (#1723; the yield rules are in
// types_stream, which imports this module, so the call rule lives here)

// The name of the stream function a call's callee spells (`next` or `close`), else an empty text.
static Str stream_callee(const types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *callee) {
    if (callee->kind != .name) return "";
    ast::Path path = callee->kind.name.path;
    if (countof(path.segments) != 2 || !same_text(path.segments[0].text, "stream")) return "";
    const types::Symbol *first = types::lookup(&c->scopes, path.segments[0].text);
    if (first != nullptr && first->kind != .module_name) return "";
    Str name = path.segments[1].text;
    if (same_text(name, "next") || same_text(name, "close")) return name;
    return "";
}

static void stream_before(Work *w, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .call)) {
        return;
    }
    ast::ExprRef args[:] = e->kind.call.args;
    push(w, e, f.expected, 6);
    for (Usize i = countof(args); i > 0; i--) {
        push(w, argument_value(args[i - 1]), nullptr, 0);
    }
}

static void stream_after(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .call)) {
        return;
    }
    ast::ExprRef args[:] = e->kind.call.args;
    Str name = stream_callee(c, e->kind.call.callee);
    if (countof(args) != 1) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`stream::", name, "` takes one argument, a `stream::Stream(T)`; pass exactly one");
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef given = typ(c, args[0]);
    if (given == invalid(c)) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    if (given->kind != .stream) {
        types::failf(c, args[0]->span, "`stream::", name, "` got a `", types::show(given),
                     "`, but a `stream::Stream(T)` is expected; pass a stream");
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    if (same_text(name, "next")) {
        finish(c, e, types::option_of(&c->table, given->element), f.expected);
    } else {
        types::set_type(c, e, c->table.void_type);
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Construction: `Point(x = 1, y = 2)` (spec "Structs, enums and building values" of #1428)

// The record a call constructs when its callee names a struct (or an alias of one), else null.
static types::TypeRef constructed_record(const types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *callee) {
    if (callee->kind != .name) {
        return nullptr;
    }
    const types::Symbol *s = types::lookup_path(&c->scopes, callee->kind.name.path);
    if (s == nullptr || s->kind != .type_name || s->type == nullptr || s->type->kind != .record) {
        return nullptr;
    }
    return s->type;
}

// Stage 0 of a construction: each field's value is checked against the field's type; the callee is not a value.
static void construct_before(Work *w, Frame f, types::TypeRef record) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .call)) {
        return;
    }
    ast::ExprRef args[:] = e->kind.call.args;
    push(w, e, f.expected, 3);
    for (Usize i = countof(args); i > 0; i--) {
        types::TypeRef wanted = nullptr;
        const ast::Expr *arg = args[i - 1];
        if (arg->kind == .named_arg) {
            const types::Slot *field = types::member(record, arg->kind.named_arg.name);
            if (field != nullptr) {
                wanted = field->type;
            }
        } else if (i - 1 < countof(record->slots)) {
            wanted = record->slots[i - 1].type;
        }
        push(w, argument_value(args[i - 1]), wanted, 0);
    }
}

// Outside the module that declares the record, a record with a private field is built only by a function of that
// module, even when every private field has a default (#1813). A record whose fields are all `pub` is built anywhere.
// False, after an error, when the record may not be built here.
static Bool private_construction(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, types::TypeRef record) {
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(record->slots); i++) {
        if (!types::field_visible(record, &record->slots[i])) {
            types::private_build(c, e->span, record);
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}

// The field an argument fills: the one at its position, or the one it names. Null, after an error, when it fills none.
export const types::Slot *argument_field(types::Checked *c, ast::ExprRef args[:], Usize i, types::TypeRef record,
                                         Str shown, Bool by_name, Usize *index) {
    const ast::Expr *arg = args[i];
    if (arg->kind != .named_arg) {
        if (by_name) {
            types::failf(c, arg->span, "a positional argument cannot follow a named one; name this field of `", shown,
                         "`");
            return nullptr;
        }
        if (i >= countof(record->slots)) {
            types::failf(c, arg->span, "`", shown, "` has ", countof(record->slots), " fields, but ", countof(args),
                         " values are given");
            return nullptr;
        }
        *index = i;
        return &record->slots[i];
    }
    Str name = arg->kind.named_arg.name;
    for (Usize k = 0; k < countof(record->slots); k++) {
        if (same_text(record->slots[k].name, name)) {
            *index = k;
            return &record->slots[k];
        }
    }
    if (countof(record->slots) == 0) {
        types::failf(c, arg->span, "`", shown, "` has no field `", name, "`; it has no fields at all");
    } else {
        types::failf(c, arg->span, "`", shown, "` has no field `", name, "`; write one of its fields, such as `",
                     record->slots[0].name, "`");
    }
    return nullptr;
}

// The values are typed: every argument fills a known field, given once, positional ones first (Python's rules).
static void construct_after(types::Checked *c, Frame f, types::TypeRef record) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .call)) {
        return;
    }
    ast::ExprRef args[:] = e->kind.call.args;
    Str shown = types::show(record);
    Bool seen[:] = mem::alloc_slice(Bool, countof(record->slots));
    Bool ok = private_construction(c, e, record);
    Bool built_here = ok;
    Bool by_name = false;
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(args); i++) {
        const ast::Expr *arg = args[i];
        Usize index = 0;
        const types::Slot *field = argument_field(c, args, i, record, shown, by_name, &index);
        if (arg->kind == .named_arg) {
            by_name = true;
        }
        if (field == nullptr) {
            ok = false;
            continue;
        }
        if (seen[index]) {
            types::failf(c, arg->span, "the field `", field->name, "` of `", shown, "` is given twice; remove one");
            ok = false;
        }
        seen[index] = true;
        if (typ(c, argument_value(args[i])) == invalid(c)) {
            ok = false;
        }
    }
    type_named_arguments(c, args);
    for (Usize k = 0; k < countof(record->slots); k++) {
        if (!built_here || seen[k] || record->slots[k].default_value != nullptr) continue;
        types::failf(c, e->span, "the field `", record->slots[k].name, "` of `", shown,
                     "` is left out and has no default; give it a value, or write a default in the struct (`",
                     record->slots[k].name, ": T = value`)");
        ok = false;
    }
    if (!ok) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    finish(c, e, record, f.expected);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Vectors: `vec(f32, 4)(1, 2, 3, 4)` (spec "Types" of #1428, #1630)

// Whether `callee` is the `vec(T, N)` of a construction: a call of the name `vec`, which no declaration owns.
static Bool is_vector_callee(const types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *callee) {
    if (callee->kind != .call) {
        return false;
    }
    const ast::Expr *name = callee->kind.call.callee;
    if (name->kind != .name) {
        return false;
    }
    ast::Path path = name->kind.name.path;
    return countof(path.segments) == 1 && same_text(path.segments[0].text, "vec") &&
           types::lookup_path(&c->scopes, path) == nullptr;
}

// The type `vec(T, N)` written as the callee of a construction: its arguments are read as a type and a count.
static types::TypeRef vector_type(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *callee) {
    if (!(callee->kind == .call) || !(callee->kind.call.callee->kind == .name)) {
        return invalid(c);
    }
    ast::Path vec_path = callee->kind.call.callee->kind.name.path;
    ast::ExprRef given[:] = callee->kind.call.args;
    if (countof(given) != 2) {
        types::failf(c, callee->span, "`vec` takes an element type and a lane count; write `vec(f32, 4)`");
        return invalid(c);
    }
    const ast::Expr *first = given[0];
    ast::Expr *second = given[1];
    if (!(first->kind == .name) || !(second->kind == .integer)) {
        types::failf(c, callee->span, "`vec` takes an element type and a lane count; write `vec(f32, 4)`");
        return invalid(c);
    }
    ast::TypeKind element_kind = {.named = {.path = first->kind.name.path}};
    ast::TypeExpr *element = ast::make_type(element_kind);
    element->span = first->span;
    ast::TypeArg args[:] = mem::alloc_slice(ast::TypeArg, 2);
    ast::TypeArg element_arg = {.type = {.type = element}};
    ast::TypeArg count_arg = {.value = {.value = second}};
    args[0] = element_arg;
    args[1] = count_arg;
    ast::TypeKind kind = {.applied = {.name = vec_path, .args = args}};
    ast::TypeExpr *written = ast::make_type(kind);
    written->span = callee->span;
    return types::resolve(c, written);
}

// Stage 0: the lanes are checked as the element type; the callee `vec(T, N)` is a type, not a value.
static void vector_before(types::Checked *c, Work *w, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .call)) {
        return;
    }
    const ast::Expr *callee = e->kind.call.callee;
    types::TypeRef made = vector_type(c, callee);
    types::set_type(c, callee, made);
    if (made == invalid(c)) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    ast::ExprRef args[:] = e->kind.call.args;
    push(w, e, f.expected, 5);
    for (Usize i = countof(args); i > 0; i--) {
        push(w, argument_value(args[i - 1]), made->element, 0);
    }
}

static void vector_after(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .call)) {
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef made = typ(c, e->kind.call.callee);
    ast::ExprRef args[:] = e->kind.call.args;
    Bool ok = true;
    if (countof(args) != made->count) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`", types::show(made), "` is built from exactly ", made->count, " values, found ",
                     countof(args), "; write one value for each lane");
        ok = false;
    }
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(args) && ok; i++) {
        const ast::Expr *lane = args[i];
        if (lane->kind == .named_arg) {
            types::failf(c, args[i]->span, "a lane of `", types::show(made), "` has no name; write the value alone");
            ok = false;
        } else if (typ(c, args[i]) == invalid(c)) {
            ok = false;
        }
    }
    if (!ok) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    finish(c, e, made, f.expected);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Index, slice and member

static types::TypeRef index_element(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, const ast::Expr *index,
                                    types::TypeRef base) {
    if (base->kind == .array) {
        if (plain_integer(index) && integer_value(index) >= base->count) {
            types::failf(c, index->span, "index ", integer_value(index), " is out of range for `", types::show(base),
                         "`, which has ", base->count, " elements; write an index below ", base->count);
            return invalid(c);
        }
        return base->element;
    }
    if (base->kind == .vector) {
        if (plain_integer(index) && integer_value(index) >= base->count) {
            types::failf(c, index->span, "index ", integer_value(index), " is out of range for `", types::show(base),
                         "`, which has ", base->count, " lanes; write an index below ", base->count);
            return invalid(c);
        }
        return base->element;
    }
    if (base->kind == .slice) {
        return base->element;
    }
    if (base->kind == .block) { // a row: a block of one dimension fewer, a slice for the last two
        if (base->count == 2) return types::slice_of(&c->table, base->element, false);
        return types::block_of(&c->table, base->element, base->count - 1);
    }
    if (base->kind == .text) {
        return types::builtin(&c->table, "U8");
    }
    if (base->kind == .pointer && base->element->kind != .void_type) {
        return base->element;
    }
    types::failf(c, e->span, "cannot index a value of type `", types::show(base),
                 "`; index an array, a slice, a `Str` or a typed pointer");
    return invalid(c);
}

static void index_after(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .index)) {
        return;
    }
    const ast::Expr *index = e->kind.index.index;
    types::TypeRef base = typ(c, e->kind.index.base);
    types::TypeRef it = typ(c, index);
    types::TypeRef bad = invalid(c);
    if (base == bad || it == bad) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    if (!is_integer(it)) {
        types::failf(c, index->span, "an index is an integer, found `", types::show(it),
                     "`; write an integer expression such as `i`");
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef element = index_element(c, e, index, base);
    if (element == bad) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    finish(c, e, element, f.expected);
}

static void slice_after(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .slice_of)) {
        return;
    }
    const ast::Expr *ends[2] = {e->kind.slice_of.start, e->kind.slice_of.end};
    types::TypeRef base = typ(c, e->kind.slice_of.base);
    types::TypeRef bad = invalid(c);
    for (Usize i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
        if (ends[i] == nullptr) {
            continue;
        }
        types::TypeRef t = typ(c, ends[i]);
        if (t != bad && !is_integer(t)) {
            types::failf(c, ends[i]->span, "a slice bound is an integer, found `", types::show(t),
                         "`; write an integer expression such as `i`");
            base = bad;
        }
        if (t == bad) {
            base = bad;
        }
    }
    types::TypeRef result = bad;
    Bool typed_pointer = base->kind == .pointer && base->element->kind != .void_type;
    if (base->kind == .array || base->kind == .slice || typed_pointer) {
        result = types::slice_of(&c->table, base->element, base->pointee_const);
    } else if (base->kind == .text) {
        result = base;
    } else if (base != bad) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "cannot slice a value of type `", types::show(base),
                     "`; slice an array, a slice, a `Str` or a typed pointer");
    }
    if (result == bad) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    finish(c, e, result, f.expected);
}

// The record a member access reads: `.` on a record, `->` on a pointer to one; reports what to write otherwise.
static types::TypeRef record_of(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, types::TypeRef base, Bool arrow) {
    Bool through_pointer = base->kind == .pointer && base->element->kind == .record;
    if (arrow && through_pointer) {
        return base->element;
    }
    // smith's own syntax has no `->`: `p.x` reads through a pointer (spec "Pointers" of #1428)
    if (!arrow && through_pointer && e->kind == .member && in_new_syntax(c, e->kind.member.base)) {
        return base->element;
    }
    if (!arrow && base->kind == .record) {
        return base;
    }
    Str found = types::show(base);
    if (arrow && base->kind == .record) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`->` needs a pointer to a record, found `", found, "`; write `.` instead");
    } else if (through_pointer) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`.` needs a record, found the pointer `", found, "`; write `->` instead");
    } else {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`", arrow ? "->" : ".", "` needs a ", arrow ? "pointer to a " : "", "record, found `",
                     found, "`; write a record such as a `struct`");
    }
    return nullptr;
}

static void member_after(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .member)) {
        return;
    }
    Str name = e->kind.member.name;
    types::TypeRef base = typ(c, e->kind.member.base);
    if (base == invalid(c)) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    if (base->kind == .tuple && !e->kind.member.arrow) {
        const types::Slot *element = types::member(base, name);
        if (element == nullptr) {
            if (countof(base->slots) > 0 && !types::is_position(base->slots[0].name)) {
                types::failf(c, e->span, "`", types::show(base), "` has no element `", name,
                             "`; write one of its names, such as `.", base->slots[0].name, "`");
                poison(c, e);
                return;
            }
            types::failf(c, e->span, "`", types::show(base), "` has ", countof(base->slots),
                         " elements, so there is no `.", name, "`; write an element from `.0` to `.",
                         countof(base->slots) - 1, "`");
            poison(c, e);
            return;
        }
        finish(c, e, element->type, f.expected);
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef record = record_of(c, e, base, e->kind.member.arrow);
    if (record == nullptr) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    const types::Slot *field = types::member(record, name);
    if (field == nullptr) {
        Str shown = types::show(record);
        if (countof(record->slots) == 0) {
            types::failf(c, e->span, "`", shown, "` has no field `", name, "`; it has no fields at all");
        } else {
            types::failf(c, e->span, "`", shown, "` has no field `", name, "`; write one of its fields, such as `",
                         record->slots[0].name, "`");
        }
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    if (!types::field_visible(record, field)) {
        types::private_field(c, e->span, record, name);
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    finish(c, e, field->type, f.expected);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Casts, sizeof, alignof and countof

static void cast_before(types::Checked *c, Work *w, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .cast)) {
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef to = resolve_written(c, e->kind.cast.type);
    const ast::Expr *operand = e->kind.cast.operand;
    types::set_type(c, e, to);
    push(w, e, f.expected, 1);
    push(w, operand, nullptr, 0);
}

// The operand is typed: asks the cast rule whether the cast may be written, then checks the result where expected.
static void cast_after(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .cast)) {
        return;
    }
    const ast::Expr *operand = e->kind.cast.operand;
    types::TypeRef to = types::type_of(c, e);
    types::TypeRef from = types::type_of(c, operand);
    types::TypeRef bad = invalid(c);
    if (hooks.cast != nullptr && from != nullptr && from != bad && to != bad && !hooks.cast(c, e, from, to)) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    finish(c, e, to, f.expected);
}

static Bool countable(types::TypeRef t) {
    return t->kind == .array || t->kind == .slice || t->kind == .text || t->kind == .block;
}

static void count_check(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, types::TypeRef t) {
    types::TypeRef bad = invalid(c);
    // `Auto` is a generic parameter or a pack not yet instantiated (#1916): its count is known at the instance.
    if (t == bad || t == c->table.automatic || countable(t)) {
        return;
    }
    types::failf(c, e->span, "countof takes an array or a slice, found `", types::show(t),
                 "`; write the name of an array or a slice");
    poison(c, e);
}

static void query_before(types::Checked *c, Work *w, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .query)) {
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef size = types::builtin(&c->table, "Usize");
    if (e->kind.query.type != nullptr) {
        types::TypeRef t = resolve_written(c, e->kind.query.type);
        Bool counting = e->kind.query.op == .count_of;
        if (counting) {
            count_check(c, e, t);
        }
        if (types::type_of(c, e) == nullptr) {
            finish(c, e, size, f.expected);
        }
        return;
    }
    const ast::Expr *operand = e->kind.query.operand;
    push(w, e, f.expected, 1);
    push(w, operand, nullptr, 0);
}

static void query_after(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .query)) {
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef size = types::builtin(&c->table, "Usize");
    types::TypeRef operand = typ(c, e->kind.query.operand);
    if (operand == invalid(c)) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    if (e->kind.query.op == .count_of) {
        count_check(c, e, operand);
    }
    if (types::type_of(c, e) == nullptr) {
        finish(c, e, size, f.expected);
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `{...}` lists and `(T){...}`

static Bool field_designator(const ast::Initializer *item) {
    if (countof(item->designators) != 1) {
        return false;
    }
    const ast::Designator *d = &item->designators[0];
    return *d == .field;
}

static Str field_name(const ast::Initializer *item) {
    const ast::Designator *d = &item->designators[0];
    if (*d == .field) {
        return d->field.name;
    }
    return "";
}

static Bool index_designator(const ast::Initializer *item) {
    if (countof(item->designators) != 1) {
        return false;
    }
    const ast::Designator *d = &item->designators[0];
    return *d == .index;
}

static const ast::Expr *index_of(const ast::Initializer *item) {
    const ast::Designator *d = &item->designators[0];
    if (*d == .index) {
        return d->index.index;
    }
    return nullptr;
}

struct Cursor {
    Usize next;
};

static types::TypeRef record_item(types::Checked *c, types::TypeRef t, const ast::Initializer *item, Cursor *cursor,
                                  ast::Span span) {
    if (field_designator(item)) {
        Str name = field_name(item);
        const types::Slot *field = types::member(t, name);
        if (field == nullptr) {
            types::failf(c, span, "`", types::show(t), "` has no field `", name, "`; write one of its fields");
            return nullptr;
        }
        if (!types::field_visible(t, field)) {
            types::private_field(c, span, t, name);
            return nullptr;
        }
        for (Usize k = 0; k < countof(t->slots); k++) {
            if (same_text(t->slots[k].name, name)) {
                cursor->next = k + 1;
            }
        }
        return field->type;
    }
    Usize limit = countof(t->slots);
    if (t->is_union && limit > 1) {
        limit = 1;
    }
    if (cursor->next >= limit) {
        types::failf(c, span, "too many values for `", types::show(t), "`, which has ", limit, " field",
                     limit == 1 ? "" : "s", "; remove the extra value");
        return nullptr;
    }
    types::TypeRef field_type = t->slots[cursor->next].type;
    cursor->next += 1;
    return field_type;
}

static types::TypeRef array_item(types::Checked *c, types::TypeRef t, const ast::Initializer *item, Cursor *cursor,
                                 ast::Span span) {
    if (index_designator(item)) {
        const ast::Expr *index = index_of(item);
        if (!plain_integer(index)) {
            types::failf(c, span, "not yet typed: a designator index that is not an integer literal");
            return nullptr;
        }
        cursor->next = integer_value(index);
    }
    if (cursor->next >= t->count) {
        types::failf(c, span, "too many values for `", types::show(t), "`, which has ", t->count,
                     " elements; remove the extra value");
        return nullptr;
    }
    cursor->next += 1;
    return t->element;
}

static types::TypeRef option_item(types::Checked *c, types::TypeRef t, const ast::Initializer *item, ast::Span span) {
    Bool designated = field_designator(item);
    Str name = "";
    if (designated) {
        name = field_name(item);
    }
    if (t->kind == .option && same_text(name, "some")) {
        return t->element;
    }
    if (t->kind == .result_type && same_text(name, "ok")) {
        return t->element;
    }
    if (t->kind == .result_type && same_text(name, "error")) {
        return t->error;
    }
    Str example = "{.some = value}";
    if (t->kind == .result_type) {
        example = "{.ok = value} or {.error = value}";
    }
    types::failf(c, span, "a `", types::show(t), "` is written ", example, ", not a list of other values");
    return nullptr;
}

// The type wanted of the value of one item of a list of type `t`, or null (reported) when the item has no place.
static types::TypeRef item_type(types::Checked *c, types::TypeRef t, const ast::Initializer *item, Cursor *cursor,
                                ast::Span span) {
    if (countof(item->designators) > 1) {
        types::failf(c, span, "not yet typed: a nested designator");
        return nullptr;
    }
    if (t->kind == .record) {
        return record_item(c, t, item, cursor, span);
    }
    if (t->kind == .array) {
        return array_item(c, t, item, cursor, span);
    }
    return option_item(c, t, item, span);
}

static Bool listable(types::TypeRef t) {
    return t->kind == .record || t->kind == .array || t->kind == .option || t->kind == .result_type;
}

static void compound_expr(types::Checked *c, Work *w, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .compound)) {
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef t = f.expected;
    if (e->kind.compound.type != nullptr) {
        t = resolve_written(c, e->kind.compound.type);
    } else if (t == nullptr) {
        types::failf(c, e->span,
                     "a `{...}` list needs a type; write `(T){...}` or give the variable a type before `=`");
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    if (t == invalid(c)) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    if (!listable(t)) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "a `{...}` list makes a struct, an array, an Option or a Result, not a `",
                     types::show(t), "`; write a value of type `", types::show(t), "`");
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    Cursor cursor = {};
    ast::Initializer items[:] = e->kind.compound.items;
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(items); i++) {
        types::TypeRef wanted = item_type(c, t, &items[i], &cursor, e->span);
        push(w, items[i].value, wanted, 0);
    }
    finish(c, e, t, f.expected);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Array literals and what only a call may hold

// `[1, 2, 3]`: where an array or a slice of `T` is expected each element is checked as a `T`; with nothing expected,
// the first element's type is the one every element must have (a hidden array of that many elements).
static void array_literal_before(types::Checked *c, Work *w, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .array_lit)) {
        return;
    }
    ast::ExprRef items[:] = e->kind.array_lit.items;
    types::TypeRef wanted = nullptr;
    if (f.expected != nullptr &&
        (f.expected->kind == .array || f.expected->kind == .slice || f.expected->kind == .vector)) {
        wanted = f.expected->element;
    }
    if (countof(items) == 0 && wanted == nullptr) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "an empty `[]` needs a type; give the variable an array or slice type before `=`");
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    push(w, e, f.expected, 4);
    for (Usize i = countof(items); i > 0; i--) {
        push(w, items[i - 1], wanted, 0);
    }
}

static void array_literal_after(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .array_lit)) {
        return;
    }
    ast::ExprRef items[:] = e->kind.array_lit.items;
    types::TypeRef expected = f.expected;
    types::TypeRef element = nullptr;
    if (expected != nullptr && (expected->kind == .array || expected->kind == .slice || expected->kind == .vector)) {
        element = expected->element;
    } else if (countof(items) > 0) {
        element = typ(c, items[0]);
    }
    Bool ok = element != invalid(c);
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(items) && ok; i++) {
        types::TypeRef t = typ(c, items[i]);
        if (t == invalid(c)) {
            ok = false;
        } else if (t != element && !(expected != nullptr && expected->kind != .automatic && element != nullptr &&
                                     expected->element == element)) {
            types::failf(c, items[i]->span, "the elements of `[...]` have one type: element ", i + 1, " is `",
                         types::show(t), "` but the first is `", types::show(element), "`; convert it with `.to(",
                         types::show(element), ")`");
            ok = false;
        }
    }
    if (ok && expected != nullptr && (expected->kind == .array || expected->kind == .vector) &&
        expected->count != countof(items)) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "`", types::show(expected), "` holds ", expected->count, " elements but this has ",
                     countof(items), "; write exactly ", expected->count, " elements");
        ok = false;
    }
    if (!ok) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    if (expected != nullptr && (expected->kind == .array || expected->kind == .slice || expected->kind == .vector)) {
        finish(c, e, expected, f.expected);
        return;
    }
    finish(c, e, types::array_of(&c->table, element, countof(items), false), f.expected);
}

// A `name = value` outside a call has nothing to name.
static void named_argument_alone(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e) {
    if (!(e->kind == .named_arg)) {
        return;
    }
    types::failf(c, e->span, "`", e->kind.named_arg.name,
                 " = ...` names an argument, so it only goes in a call: write `f(", e->kind.named_arg.name,
                 " = value)` or a bare value here");
    poison(c, e);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Lambdas

// The type of parameter `i` of a lambda: the one written, else the expected function's; null (reported) when neither.
static types::TypeRef lambda_param(types::Checked *c, const ast::Param *p, types::TypeRef signature, Usize i) {
    types::TypeRef given = nullptr;
    if (signature != nullptr && i < countof(signature->slots)) {
        given = signature->slots[i].type;
    }
    if (p->type == nullptr) {
        if (given == nullptr) {
            types::failf(c, p->span, "the lambda parameter `", p->name,
                         "` has no type; write its type, or pass the lambda where an `Fn(...)` type is expected");
        }
        return given;
    }
    types::TypeRef written = resolve_written(c, p->type);
    if (given != nullptr && written != given && written != invalid(c)) {
        types::failf(c, p->span, "the lambda parameter `", p->name, "` is `", types::show(written), "` but `",
                     types::show(given), "` is expected; write `", types::show(given), "` or leave the type out");
        return nullptr;
    }
    return written;
}

static types::TypeRef lambda_result(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, types::TypeRef signature) {
    if (!(e->kind == .lambda)) {
        return nullptr;
    }
    if (e->kind.lambda.result != nullptr) {
        return resolve_written(c, e->kind.lambda.result);
    }
    if (signature != nullptr) {
        return signature->element;
    }
    types::failf(c, e->span,
                 "the lambda has no result type; write it, or pass the lambda where an `Fn(...)` type is expected");
    return nullptr;
}

static types::TypeRef lambda_signature(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, types::TypeRef signature) {
    if (!(e->kind == .lambda)) {
        return nullptr;
    }
    ast::Param params[:] = e->kind.lambda.params;
    if (signature != nullptr && countof(params) != countof(signature->slots)) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "the lambda has ", countof(params), " parameters but `", types::show(signature),
                     "` takes ", countof(signature->slots), "; write ", countof(signature->slots), " parameters");
        return nullptr;
    }
    types::Slot slots[:] = mem::alloc_slice(types::Slot, countof(params));
    Bool ok = true;
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(params); i++) {
        types::TypeRef t = lambda_param(c, &params[i], signature, i);
        if (t == nullptr) {
            ok = false;
        }
        slots[i] = (types::Slot){.name = params[i].name, .type = t};
    }
    types::TypeRef result = lambda_result(c, e, signature);
    if (!ok || result == nullptr) {
        return nullptr;
    }
    return types::function_of(&c->table, slots, result, false);
}

static void lambda_expr(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .lambda)) {
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef expected = f.expected;
    types::TypeRef signature = nullptr;
    if (expected != nullptr && expected->kind == .fn_value) {
        signature = expected->element;
    } else if (expected != nullptr && expected->kind == .function) {
        signature = expected; // `fn(A) -> R` of smith's own syntax: the lambda has that type (#1515)
    } else if (expected != nullptr && expected != invalid(c)) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "a lambda has type `Fn(...)`, but `", types::show(expected),
                     "` is expected; write a value of type `", types::show(expected), "`");
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef function = lambda_signature(c, e, signature);
    if (function == nullptr) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef type = types::fn_value_of(&c->table, function);
    types::set_type(c, e, signature != nullptr && expected->kind == .function ? expected : type);
    types_fn::check_lambda(c, e);
    if (hooks.lambda_body == nullptr) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "not yet typed: a lambda body");
        return;
    }
    hooks.lambda_body(c, e, type);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Values: `if`, `if let`, `match` and blocks used as a value (#1511)

// The pseudo-frames of a value statement, in the `stage` of a frame whose node is the statement's expression. Stage 1
// is the last frame, which joins the branches' types.
constexpr U32 stage_enter = 100;     // a block opens its scope
constexpr U32 stage_leave = 101;     // a block, an arm or an `if let` closes its scope
constexpr U32 stage_bind = 102;      // `if let`: the scrutinee is an optional; opens a scope with the name it binds
constexpr U32 stage_arm = 103;       // `match`: checks the patterns of an arm; opens a scope with its binding
constexpr U32 stage_coverage = 104;  // `match`: every case is named once
constexpr U32 stage_statement = 105; // a statement of a block that is not an expression: J1d's hook
constexpr U32 stage_not_yet = 106;   // `message` is not typed yet

// NOLINTBEGIN(readability-identifier-naming)
enum Act {
    visit,       // read the statement: its leaves are values
    plain,       // a statement of a block that is not its value
    leave_scope, // the scope of a block or of an `if let`'s `then` ends
};
// NOLINTEND(readability-identifier-naming)

struct Step {
    const ast::Stmt *stmt;
    Act act;
};

// The static reading of one value statement: the frames to run in order, the expressions that are its values, and
// whether some branch yields none. It does not depend on any type, so the last frame reads it again.
struct Plan {
    const ast::Expr *owner;
    types::TypeRef expected;
    types::TypeRef bool_type;
    Frame out[:];
    Usize out_count;
    const ast::Expr *leaves[:];
    Usize leaf_count;
    Step steps[:];
    Usize step_count;
    Bool no_value;
};

static void emit(Plan *p, Frame frame) {
    if (p->out_count == countof(p->out)) {
        Usize capacity = countof(p->out) * 2;
        if (capacity < 16) {
            capacity = 16;
        }
        p->out = mem::realloc(p->out, capacity);
    }
    p->out[p->out_count] = frame;
    p->out_count += 1;
}

static void emit_expr(Plan *p, const ast::Expr *e, types::TypeRef expected) {
    emit(p, (Frame){.node = e, .expected = expected});
}

static void emit_pseudo(Plan *p, U32 stage, const ast::Stmt *source, Usize index) {
    emit(p, (Frame){.node = p->owner, .stage = stage, .source = source, .index = index});
}

static void emit_not_yet(Plan *p, const ast::Stmt *source, Str message) {
    emit(p, (Frame){.node = p->owner, .stage = stage_not_yet, .source = source, .message = message});
}

static void emit_leaf(Plan *p, const ast::Expr *value) {
    if (value == nullptr) {
        p->no_value = true;
        return;
    }
    if (p->leaf_count == countof(p->leaves)) {
        Usize capacity = countof(p->leaves) * 2;
        if (capacity < 8) {
            capacity = 8;
        }
        p->leaves = mem::realloc(p->leaves, capacity);
    }
    p->leaves[p->leaf_count] = value;
    p->leaf_count += 1;
    emit_expr(p, value, p->expected);
}

static void push_step(Plan *p, const ast::Stmt *stmt, Act act) {
    if (stmt == nullptr && act != leave_scope) {
        return;
    }
    if (p->step_count == countof(p->steps)) {
        Usize capacity = countof(p->steps) * 2;
        if (capacity < 16) {
            capacity = 16;
        }
        p->steps = mem::realloc(p->steps, capacity);
    }
    p->steps[p->step_count] = (Step){.stmt = stmt, .act = act};
    p->step_count += 1;
}

static Bool is_value_stmt(const ast::Stmt *s) {
    return s->kind == .expression || s->kind == .block || s->kind == .if_stmt || s->kind == .if_let ||
           s->kind == .match_stmt;
}

static Bool leaves_early(const ast::Stmt *s) {
    return s->kind == .return_stmt || s->kind == .break_stmt || s->kind == .continue_stmt;
}

static void read_block(Plan *p, const ast::Stmt *s) {
    if (!(s->kind == .block)) {
        return;
    }
    ast::StmtRef items[:] = s->kind.block.items;
    if (countof(items) == 0) {
        p->no_value = true;
        return;
    }
    emit_pseudo(p, stage_enter, s, 0);
    push_step(p, nullptr, leave_scope);
    const ast::Stmt *last = items[countof(items) - 1];
    if (is_value_stmt(last)) {
        push_step(p, last, visit);
    } else {
        push_step(p, last, plain);
        if (!leaves_early(last)) {
            p->no_value = true;
        }
    }
    for (Usize i = countof(items) - 1; i > 0; i--) {
        push_step(p, items[i - 1], plain);
    }
}

static void read_if(Plan *p, const ast::Stmt *s) {
    if (!(s->kind == .if_stmt)) {
        return;
    }
    if (s->kind.if_stmt.is_constexpr) {
        emit_not_yet(p, s, "`if constexpr` used as a value");
        return;
    }
    emit_expr(p, s->kind.if_stmt.condition, p->bool_type);
    if (s->kind.if_stmt.else_branch == nullptr) {
        p->no_value = true;
    }
    push_step(p, s->kind.if_stmt.else_branch, visit);
    push_step(p, s->kind.if_stmt.then_branch, visit);
}

static void read_if_let(Plan *p, const ast::Stmt *s) {
    if (!(s->kind == .if_let)) {
        return;
    }
    const ast::Expr *value = s->kind.if_let.value;
    const ast::Stmt *then_branch = s->kind.if_let.then_branch;
    const ast::Stmt *else_branch = s->kind.if_let.else_branch;
    emit_expr(p, value, nullptr);
    emit_pseudo(p, stage_bind, s, 0);
    if (else_branch == nullptr) {
        p->no_value = true;
    }
    push_step(p, else_branch, visit);
    push_step(p, nullptr, leave_scope);
    push_step(p, then_branch, visit);
}

static void read_match(Plan *p, const ast::Stmt *s) {
    if (!(s->kind == .match_stmt)) {
        return;
    }
    emit_expr(p, s->kind.match_stmt.value, nullptr);
    ast::MatchArm arms[:] = s->kind.match_stmt.arms;
    for (Usize a = 0; a < countof(arms); a++) {
        emit_pseudo(p, stage_arm, s, a);
        emit_leaf(p, arms[a].value);
        emit_pseudo(p, stage_leave, s, 0);
    }
    emit_pseudo(p, stage_coverage, s, 0);
}

static void read_stmt(Plan *p, const ast::Stmt *s) {
    if (s->kind == .expression) {
        emit_leaf(p, s->kind.expression.value);
    } else if (s->kind == .block) {
        read_block(p, s);
    } else if (s->kind == .if_stmt) {
        read_if(p, s);
    } else if (s->kind == .if_let) {
        read_if_let(p, s);
    } else if (s->kind == .match_stmt) {
        read_match(p, s);
    } else {
        emit_not_yet(p, s, "this statement used as a value");
    }
}

static void read_plain(Plan *p, const ast::Stmt *s) {
    if (s->kind == .expression && s->kind.expression.value != nullptr) {
        emit_expr(p, s->kind.expression.value, nullptr);
    } else {
        emit_pseudo(p, stage_statement, s, 0);
    }
}

// Reads the whole statement of `e` into a plan.
static Plan read_value(const types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, types::TypeRef expected) {
    Plan p = {.owner = e, .expected = expected, .bool_type = c->table.bool_type};
    if (!(e->kind == .stmt_expr)) {
        return p;
    }
    push_step(&p, e->kind.stmt_expr.stmt, visit);
    while (p.step_count > 0) {
        p.step_count -= 1;
        Step step = p.steps[p.step_count];
        if (step.act == visit) {
            read_stmt(&p, step.stmt);
        } else if (step.act == plain) {
            read_plain(&p, step.stmt);
        } else {
            emit_pseudo(&p, stage_leave, nullptr, 0);
        }
    }
    return p;
}

// Stage 0: the last frame goes down first, then every frame of the plan, the first on top.
static void value_before(types::Checked *c, Work *w, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    Plan p = read_value(c, e, f.expected);
    push_frame(w, (Frame){.node = e, .expected = f.expected, .stage = 1, .index = c->error_count});
    for (Usize i = p.out_count; i > 0; i--) {
        push_frame(w, p.out[i - 1]);
    }
}

static Str word(const ast::Stmt *s) {
    if (s->kind == .if_stmt || s->kind == .if_let) {
        return "if";
    }
    if (s->kind == .match_stmt) {
        return "match";
    }
    return "block";
}

// A name the pattern binds, typed as the optional's element; a name that is not a value gets the invalid type, which
// keeps the uses of it silent.
static void bind_name(types::Checked *c, Str name, types::TypeRef type) {
    if (countof(name) == 0) {
        return;
    }
    types::Symbol symbol = {.name = name, .kind = .variable, .type = type};
    if (!types::declare(&c->scopes, symbol)) {
        types::failf(c, (ast::Span){}, "`", name, "` is already declared in this scope; choose another name");
    }
}

// `if let .some(x) = o`: opens the scope of the `then` with `x` in it.
static void bind_stage(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Stmt *s = f.source;
    types::push_scope(&c->scopes);
    if (!(s->kind == .if_let)) {
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef t = typ(c, s->kind.if_let.value);
    Str name = s->kind.if_let.pattern.name;
    Str binding = s->kind.if_let.pattern.binding;
    types::TypeRef element = invalid(c);
    if (t == invalid(c)) {
        bind_name(c, binding, element);
        return;
    }
    if (t->kind != .option) {
        types::failf(c, s->span, "`if let` takes an optional, but this is a `", types::show(t),
                     "`; write a value of type `?T`, or an `if` with a comparison");
    } else if (!same_text(name, "some") && !same_text(name, "none")) {
        types::failf(c, s->span, "a `?T` is either `.some(x)` or `.none`, not `.", name, "`; write one of those");
    } else if (same_text(name, "none") && countof(binding) > 0) {
        types::failf(c, s->span, "`.none` has no value to bind; write `.none` alone");
    } else if (same_text(name, "some")) {
        element = t->element;
    }
    bind_name(c, binding, element);
}

// The place of a pattern among the cases of `t`: an enumerator's index, 0 for `.some`, 1 for `.none`; `count` when
// it names none (and `report` says so).
static Usize case_of(types::Checked *c, ast::Span span, types::TypeRef t, ast::Pattern pat, Bool report) {
    Str name = pat.name;
    if (t->kind == .option) {
        if (same_text(name, "some")) {
            return 0;
        }
        if (same_text(name, "none") && countof(pat.binding) == 0) {
            return 1;
        }
        if (report && same_text(name, "none")) {
            types::failf(c, span, "`.none` has no value to bind; write `.none` alone");
        } else if (report) {
            types::failf(c, span, "a `?T` is either `.some(x)` or `.none`, not `.", name, "`; write one of those");
        }
        return 2;
    }
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(t->slots); i++) {
        if (same_text(t->slots[i].name, name)) {
            Bool carries = t->has_payload && carries_value(t->slots[i].type);
            if (report && countof(pat.binding) > 0 && !carries) {
                types::failf(c, span, "`.", name, "` carries no value to bind; write `.", name, "` alone");
                return countof(t->slots);
            }
            return i;
        }
    }
    if (report) {
        types::failf(c, span, "`", types::show(t), "` has no enumerator `", name,
                     "`; write one of its enumerators in the arm");
    }
    return countof(t->slots);
}

static Bool matchable(types::TypeRef t) {
    return t->kind == .enumeration || t->kind == .option;
}

static Usize case_count(types::TypeRef t) {
    if (t->kind == .option) {
        return 2;
    }
    return countof(t->slots);
}

// One arm of a `match`: its patterns are cases of the scrutinee, and the scope has what the arm binds.
static void arm_stage(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Stmt *s = f.source;
    types::push_scope(&c->scopes);
    if (!(s->kind == .match_stmt)) {
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef t = typ(c, s->kind.match_stmt.value);
    ast::MatchArm arms[:] = s->kind.match_stmt.arms;
    ast::Pattern pats[:] = arms[f.index].patterns;
    Bool known = t != invalid(c) && matchable(t);
    for (Usize k = 0; k < countof(pats); k++) {
        types::TypeRef element = invalid(c);
        if (known) {
            Usize at = case_of(c, s->span, t, pats[k], true);
            if (t->kind == .option && at == 0) {
                element = t->element;
            } else if (t->kind == .enumeration && at < countof(t->slots) && t->has_payload) {
                element = t->slots[at].type;
            }
        }
        bind_name(c, pats[k].binding, element);
    }
}

// The enumerators no arm named, as "`.a`, `.b`".
static Str missing_list(types::TypeRef t, Bool seen[:]) {
    Str text = "";
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(seen); i++) {
        if (seen[i]) {
            continue;
        }
        Str name = "none";
        if (t->kind == .enumeration) {
            name = t->slots[i].name;
        } else if (i == 0) {
            name = "some(x)";
        }
        Str piece = msg(format::push("`.", name, "`"));
        if (countof(text) == 0) {
            text = piece;
        } else {
            text = msg(format::push(text, ", ", piece));
        }
    }
    return text;
}

// After the arms: every case of the enumeration or `?T` is named, and none twice.
static void coverage_stage(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Stmt *s = f.source;
    if (!(s->kind == .match_stmt)) {
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef t = typ(c, s->kind.match_stmt.value);
    if (t == invalid(c)) {
        return;
    }
    if (!matchable(t)) {
        types::failf(c, s->span, "`match` takes an enumeration or a `?T`, but this is a `", types::show(t),
                     "`; match on an enumerator, or write an `if`");
        return;
    }
    Usize total = case_count(t);
    Bool seen[:] = mem::alloc_slice(Bool, total + 1);
    Bool clean = true;
    ast::MatchArm arms[:] = s->kind.match_stmt.arms;
    for (Usize a = 0; a < countof(arms); a++) {
        ast::Pattern pats[:] = arms[a].patterns;
        for (Usize k = 0; k < countof(pats); k++) {
            Usize at = case_of(c, s->span, t, pats[k], false);
            if (at >= total) {
                clean = false;
            } else if (seen[at]) {
                types::failf(c, s->span, "`.", pats[k].name, "` is matched twice; remove one arm");
                clean = false;
            } else {
                seen[at] = true;
            }
        }
    }
    Str missing = missing_list(t, seen[0:total]);
    if (clean && countof(missing) > 0) {
        types::failf(c, s->span, "`match` over `", types::show(t), "` does not cover ", missing,
                     "; add an arm for each, since a `match` must name every case");
    }
}

// A statement of a block that is not an expression, in the scope the block opened.
static void statement_stage(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Stmt *s = f.source;
    if (hooks.statement == nullptr) {
        types::failf(c, s->span, "not yet typed: a statement inside a block that is used as a value");
        return;
    }
    hooks.statement(c, s);
}

// The pseudo-frames, by stage.
static void value_stage(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    if (f.stage == stage_enter) {
        types::push_scope(&c->scopes);
    } else if (f.stage == stage_leave) {
        types::pop_scope(&c->scopes);
    } else if (f.stage == stage_bind) {
        bind_stage(c, f);
    } else if (f.stage == stage_arm) {
        arm_stage(c, f);
    } else if (f.stage == stage_coverage) {
        coverage_stage(c, f);
    } else if (f.stage == stage_statement) {
        statement_stage(c, f);
    } else {
        types::failf(c, f.source->span, "not yet typed: ", f.message);
    }
}

// A value statement with no value: `void`, which is an error where a type is wanted.
static void finish_void(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, types::TypeRef expected) {
    if (expected != nullptr && expected->kind != .void_type) {
        Str what = "block";
        if (e->kind == .stmt_expr) {
            what = word(e->kind.stmt_expr.stmt);
        }
        types::failf(c, e->span, "this ", what, " yields no value, but a `", types::show(expected),
                     "` is expected; end every branch with a value, and give an `if` an `else`");
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    types::set_type(c, e, c->table.void_type);
}

// An unsuffixed literal takes the type of the branch before it, when it fits.
static Bool adapts(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *leaf, types::TypeRef to) {
    return literal_like(leaf) && retype(c, leaf, to);
}

// The last frame: the branches have the one type. With an expected type each was checked against it already; without
// one the first branch with a value decides, and an unsuffixed literal after it takes its type.
static void value_join(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    Plan p = read_value(c, e, f.expected);
    if (c->error_count > f.index) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    Bool no_value = p.no_value;
    types::TypeRef first = nullptr;
    for (Usize i = 0; i < p.leaf_count; i++) {
        types::TypeRef t = typ(c, p.leaves[i]);
        if (t == invalid(c)) {
            poison(c, e);
            return;
        }
        if (t->kind == .void_type) {
            no_value = true;
        } else if (first == nullptr) {
            first = t;
        } else if (f.expected == nullptr && t != first && !adapts(c, p.leaves[i], first)) {
            types::failf(c, p.leaves[i]->span, "the branches of this value have the types `", types::show(first),
                         "` and `", types::show(t),
                         "`; give them one type, for example with a conversion such as `value.to(", types::show(first),
                         ")`");
            poison(c, e);
            return;
        }
    }
    if (c->error_count > f.index) {
        poison(c, e);
    } else if (no_value || first == nullptr) {
        finish_void(c, e, f.expected);
    } else if (f.expected != nullptr) {
        types::set_type(c, e, f.expected);
    } else {
        types::set_type(c, e, first);
    }
}

static void value_after(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    if (f.stage == 1) {
        value_join(c, f);
    } else {
        value_stage(c, f);
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The walk

// `Point(a, b)`: the parser reads a name first as a type (it cannot tell it from `Pair(i32)`). When the path names a
// struct and every argument is a name or a value, it is a construction (#1813): the call is built here and checked in
// its place. Null when it is not one.
static const ast::Expr *type_value_call(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e) {
    if (e->kind != .type_value) return nullptr;
    const ast::TypeExpr *t = e->kind.type_value.type;
    if (t->kind != .applied) return nullptr;
    ast::Expr *callee = mem::alloc(ast::Expr);
    callee->span = e->span;
    callee->kind = {.name = {.path = t->kind.applied.name}};
    if (constructed_record(c, callee) == nullptr) return nullptr;
    Usize count = countof(t->kind.applied.args);
    ast::ExprRef *block = (ast::ExprRef *)mem::alloc_array_raw(sizeof(ast::ExprRef), count, alignof(ast::ExprRef));
    ast::ExprRef args[:] = block[0:count];
    for (Usize i = 0; i < count; i++) {
        const ast::TypeArg *arg = &t->kind.applied.args[i];
        if (*arg == .value) {
            block[i] = arg->value.value;
            continue;
        }
        if (arg->type.type->kind != .named) return nullptr;
        ast::Expr *value = mem::alloc(ast::Expr);
        value->span = arg->type.type->span;
        value->kind = {.name = {.path = arg->type.type->kind.named.path}};
        block[i] = value;
    }
    ast::Expr *call = mem::alloc(ast::Expr);
    call->span = e->span;
    call->kind = {.call = {.callee = callee, .args = args}};
    return call;
}

// The call is checked in the place of the type; the type takes its type afterwards (stage 1).
static void type_value_before(types::Checked *c, Work *w, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *call = type_value_call(c, f.node);
    if (call == nullptr) {
        // A type passed to a `Type` parameter (`choose(U8)`, `Pair(T)`): a compile-time value whose type the instance
        // decides, so it is `Auto` here, as the parameter that takes it is (#1916).
        types::set_type(c, f.node, c->table.automatic);
        return;
    }
    push_frame(w, (Frame){.node = f.node, .expected = f.expected, .stage = 1, .twin = call});
    push(w, call, f.expected, 0);
}

static void type_value_after(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    types::TypeRef t = types::type_of(c, f.twin);
    if (t == nullptr || t == invalid(c)) {
        poison(c, f.node);
        return;
    }
    types::set_type(c, f.node, t);
}

static Bool leaf(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (e->kind == .integer) {
        literal_integer(c, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .floating) {
        literal_float(c, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .character) {
        literal_character(c, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .string) {
        // A literal where a C function wants `const char *`: its bytes, with the zero byte after them (#1762).
        Bool as_pointer = f.expected != nullptr && f.expected->kind == .pointer &&
                          f.expected->element->kind == .integer && f.expected->element->bits == 8;
        finish(c, e, as_pointer ? f.expected : c->table.text, f.expected);
    } else if (e->kind == .boolean) {
        finish(c, e, c->table.bool_type, f.expected);
    } else if (e->kind == .null_pointer) {
        literal_null(c, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .name) {
        name_expr(c, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .variant) {
        if (is_some_value(f)) {
            return false;
        }
        variant_expr(c, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .lambda) {
        lambda_expr(c, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .try_expr) {
        not_yet(c, e, "try");
    } else {
        return false;
    }
    return true;
}

// `(a, b)`: where a tuple type is expected, each element is checked against its own element type.
static void tuple_before(Work *w, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .tuple_lit)) {
        return;
    }
    ast::ExprRef items[:] = e->kind.tuple_lit.items;
    types::TypeRef shape = nullptr;
    if (f.expected != nullptr && f.expected->kind == .tuple && countof(f.expected->slots) == countof(items)) {
        shape = f.expected;
    }
    push(w, e, f.expected, 1);
    for (Usize i = countof(items); i > 0; i--) {
        types::TypeRef wanted = nullptr;
        if (shape != nullptr) {
            wanted = shape->slots[i - 1].type;
        }
        const ast::Expr *item = items[i - 1];
        if (item->kind == .named_arg) item = item->kind.named_arg.value;
        push(w, item, wanted, 0);
    }
}

static void tuple_after(types::Checked *c, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .tuple_lit)) {
        return;
    }
    ast::ExprRef items[:] = e->kind.tuple_lit.items;
    types::Slot elements[:] = mem::alloc_slice(types::Slot, countof(items));
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(items); i++) {
        const ast::Expr *item = items[i];
        if (item->kind == .named_arg) {
            elements[i].name = item->kind.named_arg.name;
            item = item->kind.named_arg.value;
        }
        elements[i].type = typ(c, item);
        if (elements[i].type == invalid(c)) {
            poison(c, e);
            return;
        }
    }
    finish(c, e, types::tuple_of(&c->table, elements), f.expected);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Calls of a generic function (#1908, part of #1700): `alloc(i32, 10)`, `table(64)`. The arguments of the compile-time
// parameters are a type and a constant; the call is to the instance for them, made once and found after.

// The template a call names, when the callee is a plain name of a function with compile-time parameters.
static const types::Symbol *generic_callee(const types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *callee) {
    if (callee->kind != .name) {
        return nullptr;
    }
    const types::Symbol *s = types::lookup_path(&c->scopes, callee->kind.name.path);
    if (s != nullptr && s->kind == .function_symbol && s->generic && s->decl != nullptr) {
        return s;
    }
    return nullptr;
}

// The type a name with no symbol stands for when it is a builtin (`i32`, `f64`), else null with no error.
static types::TypeRef builtin_named(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *arg) {
    if (!(arg->kind == .name)) {
        return nullptr;
    }
    ast::TypeExpr *written = mem::alloc(ast::TypeExpr);
    written->span = arg->span;
    written->kind = {.named = {.path = arg->kind.name.path}};
    Usize errors = c->error_count;
    c->new_syntax = true;
    types::TypeRef t = types::resolve(c, written);
    c->new_syntax = false;
    c->error_count = errors;
    if (t == invalid(c)) {
        return nullptr;
    }
    return t;
}

// The type an argument of a `T: type` parameter stands for; null, with the error, when it is not a type.
static types::TypeRef type_argument(types::Checked *c, const ast::Param *p, const ast::Expr *arg) {
    if (arg->kind == .name) {
        const types::Symbol *s = types::lookup_path(&c->scopes, arg->kind.name.path);
        if (s != nullptr && s->kind == .type_name) {
            return s->type;
        }
        if (s == nullptr) {
            types::TypeRef t = builtin_named(c, arg);
            if (t != nullptr) {
                return t;
            }
        }
    } else if (arg->kind == .type_value) {
        c->new_syntax = true;
        types::TypeRef t = types::resolve(c, arg->kind.type_value.type);
        c->new_syntax = false;
        if (t != invalid(c)) {
            return t;
        }
        return nullptr;
    }
    types::failf(c, arg->span, "`", p->name, "` is a type argument, but this is a value; write a type such as `i32`");
    return nullptr;
}

// The constant an argument of a `const n: T` parameter stands for; false, with the error, when it is not one.
static Bool value_argument(types::Checked *c, const ast::Param *p, const ast::Expr *arg, U64 *value) {
    if (arg->kind == .integer) {
        *value = arg->kind.integer.value;
        return true;
    }
    if (arg->kind == .name) {
        const types::Symbol *s = types::lookup_path(&c->scopes, arg->kind.name.path);
        if ((s != nullptr && s->kind == .type_name) || (s == nullptr && builtin_named(c, arg) != nullptr)) {
            types::failf(c, arg->span, "`", p->name, "` is a value argument, but this is a type; write a constant");
            return false;
        }
        if (s != nullptr && s->has_value) {
            *value = s->value;
            return true;
        }
    }
    types::failf(c, arg->span, "`", p->name, "` must be known at compile time; write an integer literal or a ",
                 "compile-time parameter of the enclosing function");
    return false;
}

// Stage 0 of a call of a generic function: finds the instance, then pushes the arguments of the runtime parameters.
static void generic_before(types::Checked *c, Work *w, Frame f, const types::Symbol *template) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .call) || template->decl->kind != .function) {
        return;
    }
    ast::ExprRef args[:] = e->kind.call.args;
    ast::Param params[:] = template->decl->kind.function.params;
    if (countof(args) != countof(params)) {
        types::failf(c, e->span, "this call passes ", countof(args), " argument", countof(args) == 1 ? "" : "s",
                     " but `", template->name, "` takes ", countof(params), "; give a type or a constant for each ",
                     "compile-time parameter, then the others");
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    types::Binding given[:] = mem::alloc_slice(types::Binding, countof(params));
    Usize bound = 0;
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(params); i++) {
        if (!types::is_compile_time(&params[i])) continue;
        const ast::Expr *arg = argument_value(args[i]);
        if (types::is_type_parameter(&params[i])) {
            types::TypeRef t = type_argument(c, &params[i], arg);
            if (t == nullptr) {
                poison(c, e);
                return;
            }
            given[bound] = (types::Binding){.is_type = true, .type = t};
        } else {
            U64 value = 0;
            if (!value_argument(c, &params[i], arg, &value)) {
                poison(c, e);
                return;
            }
            given[bound] = (types::Binding){.value = value};
        }
        bound += 1;
    }
    Usize at = types::instance_of(c, template->decl, given[0:bound]);
    if (at >= c->instance_count) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef signature = c->instances[at].signature;
    types::set_type(c, e->kind.call.callee, signature);
    push(w, e, f.expected, 2);
    Usize runtime = countof(signature->slots);
    types::Binding bound_list[:] = c->instances[at].bindings;
    Usize values = countof(bound_list);
    for (Usize i = countof(params); i > 0; i--) {
        const ast::Expr *arg = argument_value(args[i - 1]);
        if (!types::is_compile_time(&params[i - 1])) {
            runtime -= 1;
            push(w, arg, signature->slots[runtime].type, 0);
        } else if (types::is_type_parameter(&params[i - 1])) {
            types::set_type(c, arg, types::builtin(&c->table, "type"));
            values -= 1;
        } else {
            values -= 1;
            push(w, arg, bound_list[values].type, 0);
        }
    }
}

// Stage 0 of a node with children: decides what is pushed.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Spread: `f(x; s...)` passes each field of the struct or named tuple `s` as the named argument of that name (#1914)

static Bool is_spread(const ast::Expr *arg) {
    if (arg->kind == .expand) return arg->kind.expand.named;
    return false;
}

// The value a spread argument spreads.
static ast::Expr *spread_value(const ast::Expr *arg) {
    if (arg->kind == .expand) return arg->kind.expand.pattern;
    return nullptr;
}

static Bool has_spread(ast::ExprRef args[:]) {
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(args); i++) {
        if (is_spread(args[i])) return true;
    }
    return false;
}

// Whether the spread's value is a name or a chain of fields of one: it is read once per field, so it must have no
// effect of its own.
static Bool plain_place(const ast::Expr *e) {
    const ast::Expr *at = e;
    while (at->kind == .member)
        at = at->kind.member.base;
    return at->kind == .name;
}

// The arguments of a call with each spread replaced by one `field = s.field` per field of `s`; null when the spread
// is refused (the error is reported).
static Bool spread_into(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *arg, ast::ExprRef out[:], Usize *count) {
    ast::Expr *pattern = spread_value(arg);
    types::TypeRef t = typ(c, pattern);
    if (t == invalid(c)) return false;
    if (t->kind != .record && t->kind != .tuple) {
        types::failf(c, arg->span, "cannot spread a value of type `", types::show(t),
                     "`; spread a struct or a named tuple after the `;`");
        return false;
    }
    if (!plain_place(pattern)) {
        types::failf(c, arg->span, "a spread reads each field of its value; spread a name, such as `opts...`");
        return false;
    }
    for (Usize k = 0; k < countof(t->slots); k++) {
        if (types::is_position(t->slots[k].name)) {
            types::failf(c, arg->span, "cannot spread a tuple whose fields have no names; name them, such as ",
                         "`(retry = 3)`");
            return false;
        }
        ast::ExprKind member = {.member = {.base = pattern, .name = t->slots[k].name}};
        ast::Expr *field = ast::expr(member);
        field->span = arg->span;
        ast::Expr *named = ast::named_arg(t->slots[k].name, field);
        named->span = arg->span;
        out[*count] = named;
        *count += 1;
    }
    return true;
}

static void spread_resolved(types::Checked *c, Work *w, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .call)) return;
    ast::ExprRef args[:] = e->kind.call.args;
    Usize room = countof(args);
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(args); i++) {
        if (!is_spread(args[i])) continue;
        types::TypeRef spread_type = typ(c, spread_value(args[i]));
        room += countof(spread_type->slots);
    }
    ast::ExprRef *block = (ast::ExprRef *)mem::alloc_array_raw(sizeof(ast::ExprRef), room, alignof(ast::ExprRef));
    ast::ExprRef all[:] = block[0:room];
    Usize count = 0;
    Bool ok = true;
    for (Usize i = 0; i < countof(args); i++) {
        if (!is_spread(args[i])) {
            all[count] = args[i];
            count += 1;
        } else if (!spread_into(c, args[i], all, &count)) {
            ok = false;
        }
    }
    if (!ok) {
        poison(c, e);
        return;
    }
    ast::Expr *made = ast::call(e->kind.call.callee, all[0:count]);
    made->span = e->span;
    types::set_method(c, e, made);
    push(w, e, nullptr, 8);
    push(w, made, f.expected, 0);
}

static void call_before(types::Checked *c, Work *w, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (!(e->kind == .call)) {
        return;
    }
    const ast::Expr *callee = e->kind.call.callee;
    const types::Symbol *template = generic_callee(c, callee);
    if (template != nullptr) {
        generic_before(c, w, f, template);
        return;
    }
    if (is_vector_callee(c, callee)) {
        vector_before(c, w, f);
        return;
    }
    if (countof(stream_callee(c, callee)) > 0) {
        stream_before(w, f);
        return;
    }
    types::TypeRef record = constructed_record(c, callee);
    if (record != nullptr) {
        construct_before(w, f, record);
        return;
    }
    if (method_shaped(c, callee) && callee->kind == .member) {
        push(w, e, f.expected, 7);
        push(w, callee->kind.member.base, nullptr, 0);
        return;
    }
    ast::ExprRef args[:] = e->kind.call.args;
    if (has_spread(args)) {
        // `f(x; s...)`: the spread values are typed first, then the call is rewritten (stage 9).
        push(w, e, f.expected, 9);
        for (Usize i = countof(args); i > 0; i--) {
            if (is_spread(args[i - 1])) push(w, spread_value(args[i - 1]), nullptr, 0);
        }
        return;
    }
    push(w, e, f.expected, 1);
    push(w, callee, nullptr, 0);
}

static void before(types::Checked *c, Work *w, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (e->kind == .type_value) {
        type_value_before(c, w, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .unary) {
        unary_before(c, w, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .binary) {
        const ast::Expr *right = e->kind.binary.right;
        const ast::Expr *left = e->kind.binary.left;
        push(w, e, f.expected, 1);
        push(w, right, nullptr, 0);
        push(w, left, nullptr, 0);
    } else if (e->kind == .assign) {
        const ast::Expr *target = e->kind.assign.target;
        push(w, e, f.expected, 1);
        push(w, target, nullptr, 0);
    } else if (e->kind == .conditional) {
        conditional_before(w, c, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .variant) {
        some_before(w, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .stmt_expr) {
        value_before(c, w, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .call) {
        call_before(c, w, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .index) {
        const ast::Expr *index = e->kind.index.index;
        const ast::Expr *base = e->kind.index.base;
        push(w, e, f.expected, 1);
        push(w, index, nullptr, 0);
        push(w, base, nullptr, 0);
    } else if (e->kind == .slice_of) {
        const ast::Expr *end = e->kind.slice_of.end;
        const ast::Expr *start = e->kind.slice_of.start;
        const ast::Expr *base = e->kind.slice_of.base;
        push(w, e, f.expected, 1);
        push(w, end, nullptr, 0);
        push(w, start, nullptr, 0);
        push(w, base, nullptr, 0);
    } else if (e->kind == .member) {
        const ast::Expr *base = e->kind.member.base;
        if (module_value(c, f, types::member_names(e))) return;
        push(w, e, f.expected, 1);
        push(w, base, nullptr, 0);
    } else if (e->kind == .cast) {
        cast_before(c, w, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .query) {
        query_before(c, w, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .array_lit) {
        array_literal_before(c, w, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .tuple_lit) {
        tuple_before(w, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .named_arg) {
        named_argument_alone(c, e);
    } else {
        compound_expr(c, w, f);
    }
}

// Stage 1 and later: the children are typed.
static void after(types::Checked *c, Work *w, Frame f) {
    const ast::Expr *e = f.node;
    if (e->kind == .type_value) {
        type_value_after(c, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .unary) {
        unary_after(c, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .binary) {
        binary_after(c, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .assign) {
        if (f.stage == 1) {
            assign_target_done(c, w, f);
        } else if (f.stage == 2) {
            assign_value_done(c, f);
        } else {
            poison(c, e);
        }
    } else if (e->kind == .conditional) {
        if (f.stage == 1) {
            conditional_branch(w, c, f);
        } else {
            conditional_after(c, f);
        }
    } else if (e->kind == .variant) {
        some_after(c, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .stmt_expr) {
        value_after(c, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .call) {
        if (f.stage == 1) {
            call_callee_done(c, w, f);
        } else if (f.stage == 7) {
            method_resolved(c, w, f);
        } else if (f.stage == 9) {
            spread_resolved(c, w, f);
        } else if (f.stage == 8) {
            types::set_type(c, e, typ(c, types::method_of(c, e)));
        } else if (f.stage == 6) {
            stream_after(c, f);
        } else if (f.stage == 5) {
            vector_after(c, f);
        } else if (f.stage == 3) {
            construct_after(c, f, constructed_record(c, e->kind.call.callee));
        } else {
            call_after(c, f);
        }
    } else if (e->kind == .index) {
        index_after(c, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .slice_of) {
        slice_after(c, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .member) {
        member_after(c, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .cast) {
        cast_after(c, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .array_lit) {
        array_literal_after(c, f);
    } else if (e->kind == .tuple_lit) {
        tuple_after(c, f);
    } else {
        query_after(c, f);
    }
}

static types::TypeRef run(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *root, types::TypeRef expected) {
    Work w = {};
    push(&w, root, expected, 0);
    while (w.count > 0) {
        w.count -= 1;
        Frame f = w.items[w.count];
        if (f.stage > 0) {
            after(c, &w, f);
        } else if (!leaf(c, f)) {
            before(c, &w, f);
        }
    }
    types::TypeRef t = types::type_of(c, root);
    if (t == nullptr) {
        poison(c, root);
        t = invalid(c);
    }
    return t;
}

// The type of `e` from `e` alone.
export types::TypeRef infer(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e) {
    return run(c, e, nullptr);
}

// The type of `e` where `expected` is wanted (null or `Auto`: as `infer`).
export types::TypeRef check(types::Checked *c, const ast::Expr *e, types::TypeRef expected) {
    return run(c, e, expected);
}
